var e=JSON.parse('[[2701,"No, a useEffect will not be called again when the value held in a useRef changes. That is because useRef returns a stable object, which does not change between renders, while React compares the references of the dependencies rather than their content. A change of ref.current therefore does not trigger the effect.","How useRef and useEffect work\\nIn React the `useRef` and `useEffect` hooks are often used together, but their interaction can cause confusion. `useRef` returns an object with a `current` property, which is preserved between renders. That object is stable — React always returns one and the same reference to it on every render. `useEffect`, meanwhile, takes an array of dependencies and compares their values (for primitives) or their references (for objects) with the previous ones. If the reference to an object has not changed, the effect does not restart.\\n\\nWhy a change of ref.current does not cause the effect\\nWhen you put `useRef()` into the array of dependencies, you pass in the ref object itself rather than its `current` property. Since the reference to the object is always the same, React sees no changes and does not call the effect again. Even if you change `ref.current`, that will not affect the comparison of the dependencies, because the reference to the object is compared rather than its content. That is a fundamental difference from state (`useState`), where a change of the value leads to a new render and to a re-examination of the dependencies.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example, on a click on the button `countRef.current` increments, but the effect will not be called again, since the reference to the `countRef` object has stayed the same. If you had used `useState`, a change of the value would have led to a repeat render and, if necessary, to a call of the effect.\\n\\nWhen is this useful\\nSuch behaviour is useful when mutable data has to be held without causing redundant renders or effects. For holding a previous value, for example, or for integration with external libraries, where a stable reference is required. If you have to respond to changes of the value, however, use `useState` or pass `ref.current` into the dependencies (but that will not work, since React does not track changes of a ref).\\n\\nIn summary\\nThe use of a `useRef` in a `useEffect`\'s dependencies does not lead to repeat calls of the effect when `current` changes. That is React\'s deliberate behaviour, which lets you avoid redundant operations. Apply it when you need a stable store of data that does not affect the effects\' lifecycle.","import React, { useRef, useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const countRef = useRef(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log(\'The effect has run\');\\n  }, [countRef]); // the dependency is the ref object\\n\\n  const increment = () => {\\n    countRef.current += 1; // a change of current will not cause the effect\\n  };\\n\\n  return <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>;\\n}"],[2702,"React is called a library because it solves only the task of rendering the UI, without imposing a structure on the whole application. Unlike frameworks, React does not manage the whole application\'s lifecycle and does not require you to follow strict rules of architecture. The developer chooses the additional tools themselves, such as the routing, the state management or the HTTP clients.","The main difference\\nA library is a set of functions that the developer calls at their own discretion. A framework, on the contrary, manages the flow of execution and calls the developer\'s code at particular moments (the inversion of control). React belongs to the first type: it provides an API for describing the interface but does not dictate how to organize the rest of the application.\\n\\nWhat React does\\nReact is responsible only for the rendering of the components and the updating of the DOM when the state changes. It does not include the routing, the work with forms, the HTTP requests or the management of global state. All of that you add through third-party libraries, such as React Router, Redux or Axios. That gives flexibility: you can combine the tools for the particular project.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHere React does not manage the application — you call render yourself. A framework such as Angular starts the application itself and controls its lifecycle.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nSuch an architecture lets you embed React in existing projects easily, use it for individual parts of a page, or create micro-frontends. It requires more responsibility from the developer for the choice and the integration of the tools, however.\\n\\nConclusion: React is a library, because it solves the narrow task of rendering and leaves the control over the architecture to the developer. That is ideal for projects where flexibility and gradual adoption are needed, but it requires a deliberate choice of the additional libraries.","// React is simply a function that you call\\nfunction App() {\\n  return <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\n// You decide how to use React yourself\\nReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')).render(<App />);"],[2703,"TanStack Query caches the requests\' data automatically. The invalidation is performed through queryClient.invalidateQueries, which marks the cache as stale and initiates a repeat request. The configuration of staleTime and gcTime lets you control the currency and the lifetime of the cache. For optimization you can use optimistic updates and refetchOnWindowFocus.","The basics of caching in TanStack Query\\nTanStack Query (formerly React Query) provides a powerful mechanism for caching server state. Every request performed through useQuery saves its result in the cache automatically. That lets you display the data instantly on repeat visits, avoiding redundant network requests. The key parameters: staleTime (the time during which the data counts as fresh) and gcTime (the time inactive data is held in the cache).\\n\\nThe invalidation of the data\\nInvalidation is the process of marking the cached data as stale. The main method is queryClient.invalidateQueries. It takes a filter by query key and marks all of the matching entries as stale, after which the active components request the data again automatically. That is critical after mutations (creation, updating, deletion).\\n\\nThe practical strategies\\n\\n- Optimistic updates: update the cache at once and then roll it back on an error.\\n- Partial invalidation: use exact keys, so as not to re-request superfluous data.\\n- refetchOnWindowFocus: update the data automatically when the user returns to the tab.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe proper configuration of the caching and the invalidation in TanStack Query improves the performance and the UX considerably. Use staleTime for data that often does not change, and invalidateQueries after mutations. That is the standard approach for modern React applications.","import { useMutation, useQueryClient } from \'@tanstack/react-query\';\\n\\nconst queryClient = useQueryClient();\\n\\nconst mutation = useMutation({\\n  mutationFn: updateUser,\\n  onSuccess: () => {\\n    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: [\'users\'] });\\n  },\\n});"],[2704,"The rendering of raw HTML is permissible only for trusted content — from your own CMS with strict filtering, for example. It is dangerous when the data comes from users or from external sources, since that can lead to XSS attacks. For safety, use sanitization (DOMPurify, for example), escaping and a Content Security Policy.","When the rendering of HTML is permissible\\nThe rendering of raw HTML from the server is permissible when the content is entirely controlled by you and contains no user input. That can be static documentation pages generated from safe templates, for example, or data from an internal CMS, where the editors go through a strict check. In such cases the risk of XSS is minimal, since an attacker cannot inject their own code.\\n\\nWhen it is dangerous\\nThe danger arises when the HTML contains data entered by users: comments, messages, profiles, uploaded files. If you insert such HTML directly through innerHTML or dangerouslySetInnerHTML, an attacker can inject a script that will steal the cookies, redirect the user or change the page. That is the classic XSS attack.\\n\\nHow to ensure the safety\\nThe first step is the sanitization of the HTML on the server and on the client. Use libraries such as DOMPurify, which remove the dangerous tags and attributes. The second step is the escaping of the output, if you are not sure the data is safe. The third is the configuration of a Content Security Policy (CSP), which limits the running of scripts. An example in React:\\n\\nOn the server (Node.js) you can use sanitize-html:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the rendering of raw HTML only for trusted content, and for user data always apply sanitization and a CSP. That will protect the application from XSS and preserve the users\' security.","import DOMPurify from \'dompurify\';\\n\\nconst safeHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(userContent);\\nreturn <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: safeHTML }} />;\\n\\nconst sanitizeHtml = require(\'sanitize-html\');\\nconst clean = sanitizeHtml(dirtyHTML, { allowedTags: [\'b\', \'i\', \'em\'] });"],[2705,"For polling in TanStack Query, the refetchInterval parameter in the useQuery hook is used. It sets the interval in milliseconds after which the data is re-requested automatically. You can also use refetchIntervalInBackground for polling while the tab is inactive. The refetch function lets you start a request by hand.","The basics of polling in TanStack Query\\nTanStack Query (formerly React Query) provides built-in support for the periodic polling of data, through the `refetchInterval` parameter. That is convenient for scenarios in which the data is updated often on the server — in dashboards, monitoring systems or chats, for example. Rather than a manual implementation with `setInterval` and the management of the state, the library takes on all of the logic of the repeat requests, the caching and the updating of the UI.\\n\\nThe key parameters and functions\\n\\n- `refetchInterval` — a number in milliseconds, setting the frequency of the automatic requests. If you pass a function, you can change the interval dynamically (stopping the polling under particular conditions, for example).\\n- `refetchIntervalInBackground` — a boolean value, permitting the polling to continue when the browser\'s tab is inactive (by default `false`).\\n- `refetch` — the function returned from `useQuery`, for starting a request by hand.\\n- `refetchOnWindowFocus` — an automatic repeat request when the window gains focus; it is useful to combine with polling.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIn this example the data will be updated automatically every 5 seconds, even if the user has switched to another tab. If the polling has to be stopped under a particular condition, you can pass a function: `refetchInterval: (query) => query.state.data?.length ? 5000 : false`.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\nPolling should be applied when the data does not change too often and a small delay is permissible. For real time it is better to use WebSockets or Server-Sent Events. It also matters to take the load on the server into account: requests that are too frequent can lead to performance problems. TanStack Query cancels the requests automatically when the component unmounts, which prevents memory leaks.\\n\\nConclusion: use `refetchInterval` in TanStack Query for the simple, reliable periodic updating of data in interfaces where currency is required without complex manual logic.","import { useQuery } from \'@tanstack/react-query\';\\n\\nfunction usePollingData() {\\n  return useQuery({\\n    queryKey: [\'items\'],\\n    queryFn: fetchItems,\\n    refetchInterval: 5000, // polling every 5 seconds\\n    refetchIntervalInBackground: true, // continue in the background\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// In the component\\nconst { data, isLoading, error, refetch } = usePollingData();"],[2706,"There are several approaches: classic CSS, preprocessors (Sass, Less) with variables and mixins, CSS modules for the isolation of the styles, utility frameworks (Tailwind) and methodologies (BEM). The choice depends on the project: for large teams modules or Tailwind are convenient; for simple sites ordinary CSS. Preprocessors add logic, but they require a build step.","The main approaches to styling\\nIn web development styles can be written in various ways, and each has its own strong points. Classic CSS is simple, but it quickly becomes hard to maintain in large projects, because of the global scope and the duplication. Preprocessors such as Sass or Less solve those problems, by adding variables, nesting, mixins and functions, which makes the code more modular and more reusable.\\n\\nCSS modules are the approach in which the styles are isolated automatically at the level of the components. Every class receives a unique hash, which rules out conflicts of names. That is especially convenient in React applications, where the components have to be independent. Tailwind CSS is a utility framework, which offers ready-made classes for fast styling without writing custom CSS. It speeds the development up, but it can lead to the markup becoming bloated.\\n\\nExamples of the code\\nAn example with the Sass preprocessor:\\n\\nAn example with CSS modules in React:\\n\\nAn example with Tailwind:\\n\\nHow to choose an approach\\nFor small projects or prototypes, ordinary CSS or Tailwind is enough. For large applications with team-based development, it is better to use CSS modules or preprocessors in combination with the BEM methodology for naming the classes. Tailwind suits the fast development of interfaces well, but it requires discipline in the use of the utilities.\\n\\nIn summary: the choice of approach depends on the project\'s scale, on the team\'s preferences and on the need for the isolation of the styles. Preprocessors and modules give more control, Tailwind speeds the process up, and classic CSS remains the basic option.","$primary-color: #3498db;\\n.button {\\n  background: $primary-color;\\n  &:hover { opacity: 0.8; }\\n}\\n\\nimport styles from \'./Button.module.css\';\\nfunction Button() {\\n  return <button className={styles.btn}>Click</button>;\\n}\\n\\n<button class=\\"bg-blue-500 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-4 rounded\\">\\n  Click\\n</button>"],[2707,"Concurrent Mode is a set of React capabilities for concurrent rendering, which lets it interrupt and reschedule the updates. startTransition is an API that marks an update as low-priority, so as not to block the user\'s input. That helps you preserve the responsiveness of the interface during heavy operations — the filtering of large lists, for example.","What is Concurrent Mode?\\nConcurrent Mode is an experimental set of React functions, which lets it break the rendering into parts and switch between the tasks without blocking the main thread. Rather than updating the whole component synchronously, React can begin the rendering, pause it if a more important update has arrived (the typing of text, for example), and then continue. That makes the interface more responsive, especially on weak devices.\\n\\nWhat is startTransition?\\nstartTransition is a function from React 18, which lets you mark a state update as a transition. Such updates count as low-priority: React can defer their rendering if there are urgent updates (presses of keys, for example). That is useful for operations that do not require an instant result — the filtering of a large list or the switching of tabs, for example.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nHere the typing of the text updates the field instantly, while the filtering of the list happens in the background, without blocking the input.\\n\\nWhen to apply it?\\nUse startTransition for updates that are not critical for an immediate response but may be expensive in computation. Concurrent Mode is switched on automatically in React 18, but startTransition is an explicit way of managing the priorities. That is especially useful in applications with large lists, with complex forms or when working with external data.\\n\\nConclusion: Concurrent Mode and startTransition help you create smooth, responsive interfaces, by separating the urgent updates from the non-urgent. Apply them when the blocking of the input during heavy operations has to be avoided, in order to improve the user experience.","import { startTransition, useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Search() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState(\'\');\\n  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    const value = e.target.value;\\n    setQuery(value); // the urgent update\\n    startTransition(() => {\\n      setResults(filterLargeList(value)); // the low-priority one\\n    });\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={query} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      <ul>{results.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2708,"For external access to a functional component\'s methods, use forwardRef for forwarding the ref and useImperativeHandle for defining the methods available to the parent. That lets you call the child component\'s methods — the focus on an input, for example — without direct access to the DOM.","The basic concept\\nIn React functional components have no instances, as class ones do, so a ref cannot be passed in directly. By means of `forwardRef`, however, a ref can be forwarded from the parent into the child component, while `useImperativeHandle` lets you define which methods and properties will be available through that ref. That is useful when a child component\'s behaviour has to be managed from outside — to focus an input field, to clear a form or to start an animation, for example.\\n\\nHow this works\\n`forwardRef` takes a function, which receives the props and the ref, and returns a React element. Inside the child component `useImperativeHandle` binds the ref to an object containing the methods you want to make public. The parent component can then call those methods through the ref, as if it were an ordinary DOM element.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe application\\nThis pattern is often used in libraries of components, when an API for managing a component has to be provided — in modal windows, tooltips or complex forms, for example. It is also useful when integrating with imperative libraries such as D3 or Canvas, where direct access to the DOM nodes is required.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `forwardRef` and `useImperativeHandle` when external access to a functional component\'s methods has to be provided. That preserves the encapsulation and lets the parent manage the child component without breaking React\'s paradigm.","import React, { forwardRef, useImperativeHandle, useRef } from \'react\';\\n\\nconst Child = forwardRef((props, ref) => {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({\\n    focusInput: () => inputRef.current.focus(),\\n    clearInput: () => (inputRef.current.value = \'\')\\n  }));\\n\\n  return <input ref={inputRef} />;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const childRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Child ref={childRef} />\\n      <button onClick={() => childRef.current.focusInput()}>Focus</button>\\n      <button onClick={() => childRef.current.clearInput()}>Clear</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2709,"The dangerous mistakes include breaking the rules of hooks (calling them in loops or conditions), wrong dependencies in a useEffect, changing the state during the render, creating new functions or objects in the dependencies, and ignoring the clean-up of the effects. That leads to memory leaks, infinite loops and an inconsistent UI.","An introduction to the dangers of hooks\\nReact\'s hooks are a powerful tool, but their improper use can lead to hard-to-catch bugs. The main risks are related to breaking the rules of hooks, to incorrect dependencies and to side effects. An understanding of those mistakes helps you write more reliable components.\\n\\nBreaking the rules of hooks\\nHooks have to be called only at the top level of a component and not inside conditions, loops or nested functions. That guarantees the order of the calls of the hooks is stable between renders. If you break that rule, React can lose the state or cause errors.\\n\\nWrong dependencies in a useEffect\\nThe dependencies determine when an effect restarts. If you state an incomplete array, the effect can use stale values (a stale closure). If you state too many dependencies, the effect will run too often. It matters to include every variable used inside the effect.\\n\\nChanging the state during the render\\nA call of setState directly in the component\'s body (rather than in an event handler or an effect) causes an infinite loop of rendering. That happens because every render causes an update of the state, which leads to a new render.\\n\\nCreating new objects in the dependencies\\nIf you state in the dependencies an object or a function created on every render, the effect will run on every render, since the reference changes. Use useCallback or useMemo in order to stabilize it.\\n\\nIgnoring the clean-up of the effects\\nEffects that create subscriptions, timers or listeners have to return a clean-up function. Without it, memory leaks and errors when the component unmounts are possible.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAvoid these mistakes by following the rules of hooks, managing the dependencies carefully and always cleaning your side effects up. That will ensure the stability and the performance of your React applications.","// Bad: a call in a condition\\nif (isVisible) {\\n  useEffect(() => { ... });\\n}\\n\\n// Good: it is always called\\nuseEffect(() => { ... });\\nif (isVisible) { ... }\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(count); // count has to be in the dependencies\\n}, []); // An error: count is not stated\\n\\n// Bad: a call of setState in the render\\nconst [value, setValue] = useState(0);\\nsetValue(value + 1); // An infinite loop\\n\\n// Good: in an event handler\\nconst handleClick = () => setValue(value + 1);\\n\\nconst fetchData = useCallback(() => { ... }, []);\\nuseEffect(() => { fetchData(); }, [fetchData]);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const timer = setInterval(() => { ... }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(timer); // The clean-up\\n}, []);"],[2710,"Use React.memo for memoizing the component, and then pass a comparison function for the props as the second argument. In the comparison function, return true if the props you want to ignore have changed, and false if the significant props have changed. That will let React skip the re-render when the unimportant props change.","The problem of re-renders in React\\nIn React a component re-renders when its state, its context or the props passed from the parent change. If a component receives many props but responds only to some of them, the redundant re-renders can reduce the performance, especially with complex computations or large lists.\\n\\nThe solution: React.memo with a custom comparison\\nReact.memo is a higher-order function, which memoizes a component. By default it performs a shallow comparison of the props and skips the re-render if all of the props are equal by reference. If you want to ignore changes of particular props, however, pass a comparison function as the second argument. That function receives the previous and the new props and has to return true if no re-render is needed and false if one is.\\n\\nIn this example the component will re-render only when the `important` prop changes. Changes of `ignored` will not cause a re-render, since the comparison function returns true when `important` has not changed.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nThis approach is useful when a component receives frequently changing props (callbacks or data that does not affect the output, for example) plus rare significant props. In a table with sorting, for example, you can ignore the prop with the sorting function if it is created afresh on every render of the parent while the important data stays the same.\\n\\nThe important nuances\\n\\n- The comparison function has to be pure and must have no side effects.\\n- If the props contain objects or arrays, compare them by deep equality by hand, since a shallow comparison will not work.\\n- Do not abuse memoization: if the component is cheap, the redundant checks can slow things down.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse React.memo with a custom comparison function when you have to control precisely which changes of the props should cause a re-render. That is an effective way of optimizing the performance in React applications, especially when working with large trees of components.","const MyComponent = React.memo(function MyComponent({ important, ignored }) {\\n  console.log(\'Render\');\\n  return <div>{important}</div>;\\n}, (prevProps, nextProps) => {\\n  // We ignore the changes of ignored and compare only important\\n  return prevProps.important === nextProps.important;\\n});"],[2711,"A matrix of access rights is a table in which the roles are matched to the actions permitted. In the interface the elements are shown or hidden on the basis of that matrix. Conditional rendering is normally used: we check the user\'s role and print the buttons or blocks needed. That improves the security and the UX, by hiding the functions that are unavailable.","What is a matrix of access rights\\nA matrix of access rights is a structure that determines which actions or resources are available to every user role. An administrator can delete records, for example, while an ordinary user can only view them. Such a matrix is normally held on the server or in the application\'s configuration and is used for the authorization.\\n\\nHow to link it to the interface\\nOn the frontend we should not rely only on the hiding of the elements — that is merely an improvement of the UX. The security is ensured on the server. To display the interface, however, we use conditional rendering: we check the user\'s role and show the corresponding elements. In React you can use a wrapper component or a hook, for example.\\n\\nThe practical aspects\\nA matrix of access rights has to be centralized and easily changeable. It is often held as an object or a table in a database. On the client you can obtain the list of permissions after the login and pass it into a context. That lets you avoid duplicating the logic and makes testing easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a matrix of access rights for a clear separation of the roles, and conditional rendering for adapting the interface. That makes the application safer and more convenient, since the user sees only the functions available to them.","const permissions = {\\n  admin: [\'create\', \'edit\', \'delete\'],\\n  user: [\'view\']\\n};\\n\\nfunction Can({ role, action, children }) {\\n  return permissions[role]?.includes(action) ? children : null;\\n}\\n\\n// The usage\\n<Can role={user.role} action=\\"delete\\">\\n  <button>Delete</button>\\n</Can>"],[2712,"The browser\'s built-in PDF viewer suits the simple cases, when a document simply has to be shown. React-PDF and similar libraries are worth using when customization of the interface, integration with React components, the handling of events, a search over the text, highlighting, annotations or control over the loading and the display is required. They give more flexibility and control, but they require additional configuration and can be heavier.","Introduction\\nBrowsers have a built-in PDF viewer, which opens when a link to a PDF file is followed. It is simple and requires no code, but its interface and behaviour cannot be changed. When a project requires a deeper integration of a PDF with the web application — in React, for example — libraries such as React-PDF are used, which wrap PDF.js and provide React components.\\n\\nWhen to use the built-in viewer\\nIf the task is simply to show a PDF document without additional functions, the built-in viewer is the optimal choice. It is fast, requires no downloading of libraries and works in every modern browser. A link to a PDF file in some documentation or a static report, for example.\\n\\nWhen to use React-PDF\\nReact-PDF is worth applying when control over the display and the interaction is needed. Here are the typical scenarios:\\n- The customization of the interface: hiding the standard buttons, adding your own controls.\\n- The integration with React\'s state: displaying the PDF depending on the application\'s data, for example.\\n- The handling of events: clicks on the pages, the highlighting of text, a search.\\n- Annotations and comments: the addition of notes over the PDF.\\n- The management of the loading: showing the progress, handling the errors.\\n- Embedding in complex layouts: in modal windows or panels, for example.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nA simple example of the usage of React-PDF for displaying a PDF with a custom navigation button:\\n\\nHere we control the navigation completely and can add any styles or logic.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the built-in viewer for the simple cases, when integration with the application is not needed. If customization, the handling of events or embedding in React components is required, choose React-PDF — it gives flexibility and control, although it does require additional dependencies and configuration.","import { Document, Page } from \'react-pdf\';\\n\\nfunction PdfViewer({ file }) {\\n  const [numPages, setNumPages] = useState(null);\\n  const [pageNumber, setPageNumber] = useState(1);\\n\\n  function onDocumentLoadSuccess({ numPages }) {\\n    setNumPages(numPages);\\n  }\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Document file={file} onLoadSuccess={onDocumentLoadSuccess}>\\n        <Page pageNumber={pageNumber} />\\n      </Document>\\n      <button onClick={() => setPageNumber(pageNumber - 1)} disabled={pageNumber <= 1}>Prev</button>\\n      <button onClick={() => setPageNumber(pageNumber + 1)} disabled={pageNumber >= numPages}>Next</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2713,"Unlike VOD, where the user controls the playback, in Live Video the UX is built around real time: minimal delay, the absence of seeking, an indication that the broadcast is live, the handling of buffering and of network failures. It matters to take the interactivity (the chat, the reactions) into account, plus the adaptation to different devices and connection speeds.","The main difference: control over the time\\nIn VOD the user manages the time completely: pausing, seeking, replaying. In Live Video the time is set by the broadcast, and the UX has to reflect that. The user cannot seek within a live broadcast, so the interface has to show explicitly that a live broadcast is in progress and provide alternative actions (a notification about a replay, for example).\\n\\nThe key aspects of the UX for Live Video\\n\\n- The latency: for interactive formats (webinars, streams with a chat) a low latency matters (less than 2–3 seconds); otherwise the dialogue with the audience breaks down.\\n- Buffering and errors: on network failures, comprehensible messages have to be shown and the reconnection has to happen automatically, without the loss of the context.\\n- The interactivity: the chat, the reactions and the polls have to be easily available but must not overload the screen.\\n- The adaptability: the video\'s quality has to change dynamically depending on the connection\'s speed, without long pauses.\\n- The indication of the state: show explicitly that the broadcast is live, how much time has passed and when it will end.\\n\\nA practical example: the handling of a reconnection\\n\\nConclusion\\nWhen designing the UX for Live Video, you have to concentrate on the minimization of the delays, the reliability of the connection and the support for the interactive elements. That is critical for platforms with live broadcasts, for online courses and for streaming services, where the user expects live participation rather than passive viewing.","// Pseudocode for handling a network failure in Live Video\\nconst video = document.getElementById(\'liveVideo\');\\n\\nfunction handleNetworkError() {\\n  showMessage(\'The connection has been lost. Reconnecting...\');\\n  // We reconnect automatically after 2 seconds\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    video.src = currentStreamUrl;\\n    video.play();\\n  }, 2000);\\n}\\n\\nvideo.addEventListener(\'error\', handleNetworkError);"],[2714,"First I assess whether the task can be solved through props or CSS variables, so as not to change the component itself. If the change is unique and should not affect the other places, I use composition or a wrapper. As a last resort I add an optional prop with a style variant. The main thing is not to break the existing behaviour and to preserve the consistency.","Introduction\\nReusable components are created for uniformity and for saving time. When the designer asks for the appearance to be changed in one particular place, it matters not to break the other usages. You have to find a balance between flexibility and stability.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Props and variants: add a prop to the component — `variant` or `size`, for example — which changes the styles. That is the cleanest way, if the change fits into the component\'s logic.\\n- CSS variables: use CSS variables for the key styles, in order to override them in the particular place through a wrapper class.\\n- Composition: create a new component, which uses the base one inside, and override the styles through a class or inline styles.\\n- Overriding through a class: if the component takes a `className`, you can add your own class and beat the styles, but that is fragile and can break when the internal structure changes.\\n\\nA practical example\\nSuppose there is a `Button` with base styles. It has to be made red in one place only.\\n\\nConclusion\\nIt is best to try props or CSS variables first, since they preserve the predictability. If the change is unique and does not fit into the design system, use composition. Avoid global overrides, so as not to create hidden dependencies.","// Button.jsx\\nfunction Button({ variant = \'primary\', className, ...props }) {\\n  return <button className={`btn btn-${variant} ${className}`} {...props} />;\\n}\\n\\n// The usage in the particular place\\n<Button variant=\\"danger\\">Delete</Button>\\n\\n// Or through CSS variables\\n<div className=\\"special-context\\">\\n  <Button className=\\"special-btn\\">A special one</Button>\\n</div>\\n\\n/* CSS */\\n.special-context .special-btn {\\n  --btn-bg: red;\\n}"],[2715,"Performing API requests directly in components is not recommended, since that mixes the presentation logic with the business logic and makes testing and reuse harder. It is better to move the requests into separate services or hooks and to manage the state through Redux or React Query. That makes the code cleaner and more scalable.","Why requests should not be made in components\\nWhen an API request is performed directly inside a UI component, the component becomes responsible not only for the display but also for the management of the data, the errors and the loading. That breaks the single responsibility principle and leads to duplicated code if several components need the same data. Besides, such components are hard to test, since network calls have to be mocked inside every test.\\n\\nHow to organize it properly\\nIt is recommended that the request logic be moved into a separate layer — into services or custom hooks, for example. That lets you reuse the logic, makes testing easier and keeps the components clean. To manage the data\'s state, Redux Toolkit or React Query is often used, which provide convenient tools for caching and synchronization.\\n\\nWhen is it permissible\\nIn small prototypes or in one-off components you can make the requests directly, but in production code it is better to follow the practices described. That matters especially in large applications, where the data is used in different places and centralized management is required.\\n\\nIn summary: move the API requests out of the components into separate hooks or services, in order to improve the readability, the testability and the reusability of the code. Use state-management libraries for the complex scenarios.","// Bad: the request inside the component\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(\'/api/user\').then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);\\n  }, []);\\n  return <div>{user?.name}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// Good: an extracted hook\\nfunction useUser() {\\n  return useQuery(\'user\', () => fetch(\'/api/user\').then(r => r.json()));\\n}\\n\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  const { data: user } = useUser();\\n  return <div>{user?.name}</div>;\\n}"],[2716,"A component has to be designed with a clear interface through props, has to perform one task, has to be independent of external state and has to support composition. It matters to avoid a hard tie to particular data and styles, so that it can be reused easily in different contexts.","The basics of designing reusable components\\nA reusable component is a building block, which can be used in different parts of an application without changes. For it to be convenient, several key principles have to be built into it. First, the component has to have a clear, predictable interface through props. That lets you pass data and settings in from outside, making the component flexible. Second, the component has to be independent: it should not address global state or external services directly but should receive everything it needs through props. That makes testing and reuse easier.\\n\\nThe principles and the examples\\nOne of the main principles is the single responsibility. A component should do only one thing but do it well. A button should be responsible only for the display and the handling of the click, for example, rather than for the business logic. Composition also matters: the components have to combine with one another easily. Use children or render props for embedding the content.\\n\\nHere the button receives its handler and its content through props, which makes it universal. It is also worth avoiding hard-wired styles and sizes; it is better to use CSS classes or CSS modules, so that the consumer can override the appearance.\\n\\nConclusion\\nBy designing components with a clear props interface, a single responsibility and support for composition, you create convenient, reusable elements. That speeds the development up, makes maintenance easier and makes the code more predictable. Apply these principles in any React project, in order to avoid duplication and chaos.","// An example of a reusable button\\nfunction Button({ onClick, children, variant = \'primary\' }) {\\n  return (\\n    <button className={`btn btn-${variant}`} onClick={onClick}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The usage in different places\\n<Button onClick={handleSave}>Save</Button>\\n<Button onClick={handleCancel} variant=\\"secondary\\">Cancel</Button>"],[2717,"You have to create a configuration for the form (a schema) with the types of the fields, plus a mapping of the types to components. The frontend receives the schema, iterates over the fields and renders the corresponding components. For the validation and the submission of the data, generalized logic is used, which depends on the field\'s type. That lets you add new types without changing the main code.","The main idea\\nA dynamic form is a form whose structure is determined by the data obtained from the server. Rather than hard-coded fields, we describe a schema: an array of objects, each of which contains the field\'s type, its name, its label, its validation rules and other parameters. The frontend interprets that schema and renders the components needed.\\n\\nThe architecture of the solution\\nThe key element is a component registry. That is an object in which the key is the field\'s type as a string (\'text\', \'select\', \'date\', for example) and the value is the React component that can display and manage that field. During the render we go through the schema and, for every field, take the component out of the registry.\\n\\nThe validation and the submission\\nEvery field component can contain its own validation logic, or we use a common validation schema, in which the rules are described in the field\'s data (required, minLength, pattern, for example). On the submission we gather all of the values and send them to the server. For complex forms you can use libraries such as React Hook Form or Formik, which work well with dynamic schemas.\\n\\nThe advantages and the application\\nSuch an approach is widely applied in survey builders, in admin panels and in systems with configurable business processes. It lets you change the form without redeploying the frontend, add new types of field by extending the registry, and maintain uniformity easily.\\n\\nIn summary: use the schema-driven approach with a component registry when the form\'s structure depends on external data. That gives flexibility and scalability and makes maintenance easier.","const fieldRegistry = {\\n  text: TextField,\\n  select: SelectField,\\n  date: DateField,\\n  checkbox: CheckboxField\\n};\\n\\nfunction DynamicForm({ schema }) {\\n  const [values, setValues] = useState({});\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      {schema.map(field => {\\n        const Component = fieldRegistry[field.type];\\n        return <Component\\n          key={field.name}\\n          field={field}\\n          value={values[field.name]}\\n          onChange={(val) => setValues({...values, [field.name]: val})}\\n        />;\\n      })}\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2718,"Begin with an audit of the existing interfaces and the extraction of the repeated patterns. Create design tokens for the colours, the typography and the spacing. Develop the base components (buttons, fields, cards) with accessibility in mind. Document every component with examples and rules of use. Adopt it in stages, beginning with the new projects, and ensure feedback from the teams.","An introduction to design systems\\nA design system is a set of reusable components, rules and tokens, which ensure the consistency of the interfaces and speed the development up. Creating one from scratch requires a strategic approach, so that the system is useful and accepted by the teams.\\n\\nStep 1: the audit and the inventory\\nGather all of the existing screens and extract the repeated elements: the buttons, the forms, the navigation. Determine which patterns are used most often and where there are discrepancies. That will help you form the priorities for the first components.\\n\\nStep 2: the design tokens\\nTokens are variables holding the base values (the colours, the fonts, the spacing). They let you change the theme without rewriting the code. An example in CSS:\\n\\nThe tokens have to be described in the documentation and used in all of the components.\\n\\nStep 3: the creation of the components\\nBegin with the atomic elements: buttons, inputs, icons. Then combine them into more complex ones (cards, modal windows). Every component has to support the states (hover, disabled, error) and has to be accessible (the aria attributes). An example of a button in React:\\n\\nStep 4: the documentation and the adoption\\nCreate a platform (Storybook, for example) with examples and a description of every component\'s API. Adopt the system in stages: first in the new features, then gradually refactor the old ones. Organize channels of feedback (a chat, regular meetings) for gathering the problems and the improvements.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA design system pays for itself through the reduction of the development time and the improvement of the UI\'s quality. Begin small, involve the teams and develop the system constantly on the basis of the real needs.",":root {\\n  --color-primary: #0052cc;\\n  --spacing-md: 16px;\\n  --font-size-base: 16px;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Button({ variant = \'primary\', children }) {\\n  return <button className={`btn btn-${variant}`}>{children}</button>;\\n}"],[2719,"It is better to use one universal component for all of the types of field, which takes a configuration (the type, the name, the value, the handlers). The dynamic rendering is implemented through an array of configurations, which is mapped into components. That makes maintaining and extending the forms easier.","Introduction\\nWhen developing forms in React, a question often arises: should you create separate components for every type of field (a checkbox, an input, a select) or one universal one? The universal approach is preferable, since it reduces the duplication of code and makes the addition of new types of field easier.\\n\\nA universal field component\\nCreate a `FormField` component, which takes a configuration object and renders the element needed on the basis of the type. For example:\\n\\nThat component encapsulates the logic of the display and of the handling of the events, keeping the form\'s code clean.\\n\\nThe dynamic rendering of a set of fields\\nFor the dynamic rendering, hold the array of the fields\' configurations in the state or in a separate file. Then use `map` for generating the components:\\n\\nSuch an approach lets you add, remove or change the fields easily without changing the form component\'s structure.\\n\\nThe advantages and the application\\nA universal component with a configuration is especially useful when creating dynamic forms (survey builders, profile settings, for example). It makes testing and reuse easier. For complex fields with unique logic, however, a separate component may be required, but it can be embedded in the universal one by extending the configuration.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse one universal component for the standard fields plus dynamic rendering through an array of configurations. That reduces the complexity and improves the maintainability and the scalability of the forms in React applications.","function FormField({ field, value, onChange }) {\\n  switch (field.type) {\\n    case \'checkbox\':\\n      return <input type=\\"checkbox\\" checked={value} onChange={onChange} />;\\n    case \'text\':\\n      return <input type=\\"text\\" value={value} onChange={onChange} />;\\n    case \'select\':\\n      return <select value={value} onChange={onChange}>{field.options.map(...)}</select>;\\n    default:\\n      return null;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst fieldsConfig = [\\n  { name: \'agree\', type: \'checkbox\', label: \'I agree\' },\\n  { name: \'username\', type: \'text\', label: \'The name\' },\\n  { name: \'role\', type: \'select\', options: [\'admin\', \'user\'] }\\n];\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const [values, setValues] = useState({});\\n  const handleChange = (name, value) => setValues(prev => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      {fieldsConfig.map(field => (\\n        <FormField key={field.name} field={field} value={values[field.name]} onChange={(e) => handleChange(field.name, e.target.value)} />\\n      ))}\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2720,"It is better to build in the minimal necessary set of props, covering the current and the obvious near-term needs, but with a thought-out architecture allowing the API to be extended easily. Redundant props complicate the component, increase the surface of the testing and create confusion. It is optimal to use composition and default values, in order to preserve the flexibility without overloading.","An introduction to the problem\\nThe design of a design system\'s components\' API is always a search for a compromise. On the one hand, you want to provide for every possible scenario of use, so that the developers do not write custom styles. On the other, every superfluous prop makes the component heavier and harder to maintain and to test. The classic YAGNI (You Aren\'t Gonna Need It) principle says that functionality should not be added before it is really needed. In design systems, however, it matters to think a step ahead, since changing the API after a mass adoption is a costly operation.\\n\\nThe practical approach\\nIt is recommended that you begin with a minimal set of props, which cover the real cases from the current mock-ups and requirements. The architecture has to be flexible, however: use composition (children, render props) and default values, in order to extend the functionality without breaking changes. Rather than adding an `iconPosition` prop, for example, it is better to let the icon be passed in as part of the children, or to use slots.\\n\\nThe key principles\\n\\n- Minimalism: every prop has to have a clear purpose and has to be used in at least one real scenario.\\n- Composition: prefer nesting and children to a great many configuration props.\\n- Defaults: set sensible default values, in order to make the usage simpler.\\n- Documentation: describe every prop and its effect on the behaviour clearly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nIn the end the best approach is a balance: build in only what is needed now, but design the component so that it can be extended easily without breaking changes. That lets you preserve the simplicity and the speed of the development while ensuring the design system\'s long-term durability.","// Bad: too many props for a simple button\\n<Button\\n  variant=\\"primary\\"\\n  size=\\"large\\"\\n  icon={<Icon />}\\n  iconPosition=\\"left\\"\\n  loading\\n  isFullWidth\\n  rounded\\n  shadow\\n  uppercase\\n>\\n  Click me\\n</Button>\\n\\n// Good: a minimal set, extensible through children\\n<Button variant=\\"primary\\">\\n  <Icon /> Click me\\n</Button>"],[2721,"Client components in Next.js are rendered on the server for the generation of the initial HTML and are then hydrated in the browser. That means their HTML markup is created on the server, but the interactivity and the handling of the events work only once the JavaScript has loaded in the browser.","How client components work in Next.js\\nIn Next.js client components are the components that are marked with the `\'use client\'` directive. They take part in the server rendering: the server generates their HTML markup, which is sent to the client. That matters for the SEO and for a fast initial load, since the user sees the content before the JavaScript runs.\\n\\nOnce the HTML has loaded in the browser, Next.js performs the hydration: the component\'s JavaScript code is downloaded and bound to the existing markup, adding the interactivity (the event handlers, the state, the effects). Before the hydration a client component is displayed as static HTML.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThis component is rendered on the server as `Count: 0` and then becomes interactive in the browser.\\n\\nConclusion\\nClient components do not run only in the browser — they are rendered on the server for the initial HTML, but their logic and interactivity work exclusively on the client. That combination provides a fast load together with a full interaction.","// app/counter.tsx\\n\'use client\';\\nimport { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nexport default function Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n      Count: {count}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[2722,"If many client components are placed in the root layout, the whole layout will become a client one. That will increase the size of the JavaScript bundle, slow the initial load down and make the SEO worse, since the server rendering will be limited. It is better to keep the layout on the server and to move the client components closer to the place where they are used.","What happens when \'use client\' is used in a layout\\nIn Next.js (the App Router) the root layout is a server component by default. When you add client components with the \'use client\' directive to it, the whole layout and all of its child components become part of the client bundle. That means the JavaScript for those components will be sent to the browser, increasing the size of the code downloaded.\\n\\nThe effect on the performance\\nA large number of client components in a layout leads to the following problems:\\n- An increase in the initial loading time (the TTI), because of the larger volume of JS.\\n- A reduction in the speed of the indexing by search engines, since part of the content is rendered on the client.\\n- Caching and optimization on the server become more complicated.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nInstead it is better to leave the layout on the server and to move the client components into the particular pages or child components where they really are needed.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \'use client\' only where interactivity is required (event handlers, state hooks). Keep the layout and the static parts on the server, in order to minimize the bundle and improve the performance.","// app/layout.tsx\\nimport ClientComponent from \'./ClientComponent\';\\n\\nexport default function RootLayout({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <html>\\n      <body>\\n        <ClientComponent /> {/* it makes the layout a client one */}\\n        {children}\\n      </body>\\n    </html>\\n  );\\n}"],[2723,"Yes, such situations exist. If the application is an internal tool, an admin panel or a dashboard with closed access, where SEO does not matter and the users are already authorized, an ordinary SPA in React will be simpler and cheaper. Next.js adds complexity with the server rendering, the deployment and the configuration, which is not justified without public content and without requirements on the speed of the first load.","When Next.js is superfluous\\nNext.js is a framework based on React, which adds server-side rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG) and other capabilities. Those functions are not always needed, however. If your application is an internal control panel, an analytical dashboard or a tool for employees, where the access is limited by the authorization, the search engines do not index the pages and the users do not depend on SEO. In that case a classic SPA in React with Create React App or Vite will be simpler: fewer dependencies, a faster build, easier deployment to a static host.\\n\\nThe key criteria for the choice\\n\\n- Public content and SEO — Next.js is needed.\\n- A high speed of the first load for marketing pages — SSG is useful.\\n- Frequently updated data and dynamics — an SPA can be more convenient.\\n- The team and the infrastructure — if there is no experience with server-side code, an SPA lowers the barrier to entry.\\n\\nAn example: when an SPA is enough\\n\\nHere there is no need for SSR, because the data arrives after the login and SEO is not required. Such an application can be deployed to Netlify or Vercel as static files.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse Next.js when SEO, the fast delivery of public content or isomorphic applications are needed. For internal tools, admin panels and applications with closed access, an ordinary SPA in React is the more rational choice: it is simpler to develop and to maintain.","// A React SPA (Vite) — a simple application for internal use\\nimport React, { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Dashboard() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState([]);\\n  // the loading of the data from the API after the authorization\\n  return <div>The control panel</div>;\\n}\\n\\nexport default Dashboard;"],[2724,"Yes, there is a point in wrapping a filtered list in a useMemo if the filtering is performed over a large array of data and happens on every render. useMemo caches the result of the computation and recomputes it only when the dependencies change, which reduces the load on the render. For small lists or simple operations, however, that can be superfluous, since useMemo itself also has a small overhead.","What useMemo is, and what it is for\\nuseMemo is a React hook, which lets you memoize (cache) the result of a computation. It takes a function plus an array of dependencies. React recomputes the value only when one of the dependencies changes. That helps you avoid repeat costly computations on every render of the component.\\n\\nWhen to wrap a filtered list in a useMemo\\nIf you have a large array of data (thousands of items, for example) and you filter it on the basis of some state (a search query, for example), then without a useMemo the filtering will be performed on every render of the component, even if the state it depends on has not changed. That can lead to reduced performance, especially if the filtering is complex or the array is very large.\\n\\nAn example: you have a list of products, and the user types text in a search field. Every time the user types, the state of the search query updates and the component repaints. Without a useMemo the filtering will be performed afresh on every render, even if the query has not changed (because of a change of other state, for example). With a useMemo the result of the filtering will be recomputed only when the query or the original array changes.\\n\\nWhen useMemo is not needed\\nIf the array is small (up to 100 items, for example) and the filtering is simple (a comparison of strings, for example), then the overhead of the useMemo itself can be greater than the benefit of the caching. React also optimizes the rendering, and in most cases for small data the filtering creates no noticeable problems. Besides, if the filtering is performed only once, when the component mounts, and does not depend on changing state, a useMemo will give no advantage.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse useMemo for filtered lists when you have large arrays of data and the filtering depends on changing state. That will help you avoid redundant computation and improve the performance. For small lists or simple operations the memoization can be superfluous — in such cases it is better to rely on React\'s standard rendering.","import { useMemo, useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction ProductList({ products }) {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n  const filteredProducts = useMemo(() => {\\n    return products.filter(product =>\\n      product.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())\\n    );\\n  }, [products, query]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ul>\\n        {filteredProducts.map(product => (\\n          <li key={product.id}>{product.name}</li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2725,"The filters\' state is better held in the URL if they affect the page\'s display and have to be available for sharing or for going back. For internal filters that do not affect the URL, you can use the application\'s local state. The passing into the API normally happens through query parameters, which are formed from the filters\' state.","An introduction to the management of filters\\nFilters are a key element of a user interface, which lets you narrow a selection of data down. Holding their state properly is critically important for the user\'s convenience and for the application working correctly. The main question is where to hold the state: in the URL or in global state (Redux or React Context, for example).\\n\\nHolding it in the URL\\nWhen the filters affect the page\'s content and have to be reproducible (when a link is sent to another person, for example), they should be held in the URL\'s query parameters. That gives several advantages: the user can share a link with the filters applied, the \\"back\\" button works naturally, and the state is easy to synchronize with the server when the page loads. An example: `https://example.com/products?category=electronics&price=100-500`.\\n\\nHolding it in the application\'s state\\nIf the filters are temporary in nature and should not affect the URL (a filter in a modal window, or settings that do not have to be shared, for example), it is better to use the component\'s local state or global state. That makes the code simpler and avoids cluttering the URL with superfluous parameters.\\n\\nPassing the filters into the API\\nWhen sending a request to an API, the filters are normally transformed into query parameters. In React with the use of fetch, for example:\\n\\nIf the state is held in the URL, you can read the parameters directly from `window.location.search` and pass them into the request. When using React Router it is convenient to use the `useSearchParams` hook.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- For public pages (catalogues, a search) — hold the filters in the URL.\\n- For internal elements (control panels, temporary settings) — use local state.\\n- Synchronize the state with the URL through libraries such as `react-router-dom`, or by hand through `history.pushState`.\\n- When forming the request, always use URLSearchParams for the proper encoding.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of where to hold it depends on the context: if the filters have to be reproducible and available for sharing, use the URL; otherwise the application\'s state. That provides a better user experience and makes the integration with the API easier.","const fetchProducts = async (filters) => {\\n  const params = new URLSearchParams(filters).toString();\\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/products?${params}`);\\n  return response.json();\\n};\\n\\n// The usage\\nconst filters = { category: \'electronics\', price: \'100-500\' };\\nfetchProducts(filters);"],[2726,"When the state updates, React creates a new virtual DOM tree, compares it with the previous one (diffing) and computes the minimal changes. React then applies those changes to the real DOM through batched updates, which improves the performance. Direct manipulations of the DOM are not performed directly; everything happens through the virtual layer.","How React updates the DOM\\nWhen you call setState or use hooks, React marks the component as changed and schedules a repeat render. Rather than changing the real DOM immediately, React builds a new virtual DOM tree, which is a lightweight JavaScript representation of the interface. That tree is compared with the previous one by means of the reconciliation algorithm, which finds the differences and forms a list of the minimal operations for updating the real DOM.\\n\\nThe process of the update\\n\\n- The creation of a new virtual tree on the basis of the current state.\\n- The comparison with the previous virtual tree (diffing).\\n- The computation of a patch — a set of changes (the addition, the removal, the updating of nodes).\\n- The application of the patch to the real DOM in one batch, which reduces the number of repaints.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of the virtual DOM lets React update the interface efficiently, avoiding costly operations on the real DOM. That is especially useful in large applications with frequent changes of state, where the performance is critical.","function Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;\\n}\\n// On a click React creates a new virtual tree with count=1,\\n// compares it with the previous one (count=0) and updates only the button\'s text."],[2727,"useCallback returns a stable reference to a function if the dependencies have not changed. That lets child components wrapped in React.memo skip a repeat render, since the props (the functions) stay equal by reference. Without useCallback a new function is created on every render, which breaks the memoization.","The problem: new references to the functions\\nIn React, on every render of a component all of the functions declared inside it are created afresh. That means that even if the function\'s logic has not changed, the reference to it becomes new. When such a function is passed into a child component wrapped in React.memo, the comparison of the props by reference shows that the function has changed, and the child component renders again, even if its other props and its state have not changed.\\n\\nThe solution: useCallback\\nThe useCallback hook takes a function plus an array of dependencies. It returns a memoized version of the function, which stays the very same reference until the dependencies change. That lets child components with React.memo determine properly that the function prop has not changed and skip the redundant render.\\n\\nIn the example above, when the user types text in the input, the text state changes, causing a render of Parent. Without the useCallback the handleClick function would be recreated and Child would render again. With the useCallback the reference to handleClick is stable, so Child does not render needlessly.\\n\\nWhen to use it\\nuseCallback is useful when a function is passed into memoized child components or is used in effects (useEffect) as a dependency. You should not wrap every function, however — that adds the overhead of comparing the dependencies. Apply it deliberately, when there is a real performance problem.\\n\\nIn summary: useCallback helps you avoid redundant renders by preserving a stable reference to a function, which is critical for the memoization of the child components and for optimizing the performance in React applications.","import React, { useCallback, useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [text, setText] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n  // Without a useCallback: a new function on every render\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []); // empty dependencies — the function is stable\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} />\\n      <input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log(\'Child render\');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});"],[2728,"The main ways of sending HTTP requests from the frontend are the built-in fetch API and the third-party axios library. There is also the outdated XMLHttpRequest, which is rarely used directly. fetch is simple and built into the browser, but axios provides convenient interceptors and the automatic handling of JSON. The choice depends on the project: for simple tasks fetch is enough, while for complex applications axios is often taken.","An introduction to HTTP requests on the frontend\\nWhen the browser has to obtain data from a server or send data to it, HTTP requests are used. On the frontend that is done asynchronously, so as not to block the interface. There are several tools, each with its own strong points.\\n\\nThe browser\'s built-in capabilities\\nThe simplest way is to use the global `fetch` object, which returns a Promise. It is supported by every modern browser and requires no installation of additional packages. For the handling of the errors and the transformation of the response into JSON, however, you have to write additional checks.\\n\\nThe older approach is `XMLHttpRequest`, but it is cumbersome and is rarely used directly, more often through wrappers.\\n\\nThe popular axios library\\nAxios is a third-party library, which works both in the browser and in Node.js. It provides a convenient API, transforms JSON automatically, and supports the cancellation of requests and interceptors for handling tokens or for logging. An example:\\n\\nThere are also other libraries — `ky` (a light wrapper over fetch) or `superagent`, for example — but they are less widespread.\\n\\nHow to choose?\\nIf the project is small and requires no complex request logic, the built-in fetch is enough. For large applications with a great many API calls, where interceptors, timeouts and the cancellation of requests are needed, axios is more convenient. In the modern frameworks, such as React, axios is often used together with hooks or with state-management libraries.\\n\\nIn summary\\nA knowledge of these tools matters for any frontend developer. fetch is the basic standard, axios the popular superstructure, which makes the work easier. In real projects the choice depends on the functional requirements and on the team\'s preferences.","fetch(\'/api/users\')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(\'Network error\');\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(err => console.error(err));\\n\\naxios.get(\'/api/users\')\\n  .then(response => console.log(response.data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));"],[2729,"For the export to Excel, libraries such as SheetJS (xlsx) or ExcelJS are used, which create .xlsx files in the browser. For PDF, jsPDF or pdfmake is applied, forming the document programmatically or from HTML. You can also send a request to the server, which generates the file, but the frontend approach is faster and does not load the server. The choice depends on the complexity of the data and on the need for styling.","The main approaches to the export\\nThe export of data on the frontend lets the user download a file without addressing the server, which speeds the work up and reduces the load. There are two popular formats: Excel for tabular data and PDF for documents with an arbitrary layout. For each format there are libraries that work directly in the browser.\\n\\nThe export to Excel\\nTo create Excel files, the SheetJS (xlsx) library is used most often. It lets you transform an array of objects or JSON into a worksheet and save it as an .xlsx. An example of the code:\\n\\nThis approach is simple and suits most cases. If complex styles, formulas or several sheets are needed, you can use ExcelJS, which provides a broader API.\\n\\nThe export to PDF\\nFor PDF, jsPDF or pdfmake is often applied. jsPDF lets you add text and images directly, while pdfmake supports a declarative description of the document. An example with jsPDF:\\n\\nIf an HTML table has to be exported to a PDF, you can use html2canvas for taking a screenshot and inserting it into the PDF, but that is less flexible and heavier in performance.\\n\\nWhen to choose the frontend and when the server\\nFrontend export is convenient for small volumes of data and for simple reports. If the data is large (hundreds of thousands of rows) or complex computation is required, it is better to generate the file on the server, so as not to block the browser. Server-side export also lets you use powerful templating engines and protect the data from unauthorized access.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse frontend libraries for the fast export of small and medium volumes of data, when the interactivity and an instant reaction matter. For heavy reports or security requirements, prefer the server-side generation of the files.","import * as XLSX from \'xlsx\';\\n\\nconst data = [\\n  { name: \'Ivan\', age: 30 },\\n  { name: \'Maria\', age: 25 }\\n];\\n\\nconst worksheet = XLSX.utils.json_to_sheet(data);\\nconst workbook = XLSX.utils.book_new();\\nXLSX.utils.book_append_sheet(workbook, worksheet, \'Data\');\\nXLSX.writeFile(workbook, \'report.xlsx\');\\n\\nimport { jsPDF } from \'jspdf\';\\n\\nconst doc = new jsPDF();\\ndoc.text(\'The report for the month\', 10, 10);\\ndoc.text(\'Sales: 100,000\', 10, 20);\\ndoc.save(\'report.pdf\');"],[2730,"A system of roles on the frontend is normally built on the basis of the data about the user obtained from the server (a JWT, for example). Hold the roles and the rights in the application\'s state (Redux, Context). Use protected routes (route guards) for limiting the access to the pages, and conditional rendering for hiding the elements. It matters to remember that frontend checks are not security but only improve the UX; the real authorization is always on the server.","The basics of role-based access on the client\\nRoles and access rights on the frontend are needed for managing the visibility of the interface and the navigation depending on the user\'s level of privilege. Normally, after the authentication the server returns a token (a JWT, for example), in which the user\'s identifier and their roles are encoded. The frontend decodes that token and saves the information about the roles in global state (Redux, Zustand, the Context API).\\n\\nThe protection of the routes\\nTo limit the access to the pages, so-called route guards are applied. In React Router that is implemented through a wrapper component, which checks for the presence of the role needed before rendering the target page. If the role does not fit, the user is redirected to the login page or to a 403 error page.\\n\\nThe conditional rendering of the elements\\nTo hide buttons, links or whole blocks, a check of the rights in the JSX is used. A delete button is available only to moderators, for example. That is done through a hasPermission function, which compares the user\'s rights with the ones required.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\nHold the roles in a separate module, in order to avoid duplicated strings. Use constants for the names of the roles and the rights. For complex systems you can create a usePermissions hook, which returns the checking functions. It matters not to trust the frontend in matters of security: all of the critical actions have to be checked on the server.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA system of roles on the frontend improves the user experience and makes the navigation easier, but it does not replace server-side authorization. Apply it together with API checks, in order to ensure the security and the proper behaviour of the interface.","const ProtectedRoute = ({ role, children }) => {\\n  const { user } = useAuth();\\n  if (!user) return <Navigate to=\\"/login\\" replace />;\\n  if (role && !user.roles.includes(role)) {\\n    return <Navigate to=\\"/403\\" replace />;\\n  }\\n  return children;\\n};\\n\\n// The usage\\n<Route path=\\"/admin\\" element={\\n  <ProtectedRoute role=\\"admin\\">\\n    <AdminPanel />\\n  </ProtectedRoute>\\n} />\\n\\nconst canDelete = hasPermission(user, \'delete_post\');\\n{canDelete && <button onClick={handleDelete}>Delete</button>}"],[2731,"In Nuxt SSR you can use caching (of the pages, the components, the API), code splitting and lazy loading for reducing the loading time. It also matters to optimize the server side — by means of a CDN and compression, for example. These methods reduce the load on the server and speed the display of the content up.","An introduction to the optimization of SSR in Nuxt\\nSSR applications in Nuxt deliver finished HTML to the client, which improves the SEO and the first impression. Without optimization, however, the server can respond slowly while the pages take a long time to load. The main problems: redundant requests to the API, the repeat generation of identical pages, and a large bundle size. The optimization is aimed at cutting the server\'s response time down and reducing the volume of the data transferred.\\n\\nCaching\\nCaching is the key tool. In Nuxt you can cache whole pages by means of `nuxt.config` and middleware. For static pages, for example, `cache: true` is used in `render`. For dynamic data, caching at the level of the API or of the components is applied, through `useAsyncData` with the `stale` parameter.\\n\\nIt is also useful to cache the responses of external APIs by means of Redis or in memory, so as not to make repeat requests on every render.\\n\\nCode splitting and lazy loading\\nNuxt splits the code by page automatically, but it can be improved: use dynamic imports for the components and libraries that are not needed at once. Heavy charts or third-party widgets are loaded only when they appear in the viewport, for example.\\n\\nThat reduces the initial bundle and speeds the interactivity up.\\n\\nThe optimization of the server side\\nUse a CDN for delivering the static files, and compression (gzip/brotli) for reducing the traffic. In Nuxt you can configure the `compression` middleware. It also matters to avoid heavy operations on the server: move the computation into background tasks, or use edge rendering.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe optimization of SSR in Nuxt is a combination of caching, code splitting and the proper configuration of the server. Apply these methods when your application is experiencing a high load or responding slowly, in order to improve the user experience and reduce the infrastructure costs.","// nuxt.config.js\\nexport default {\\n  render: {\\n    cache: {\\n      max: 1000,\\n      maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 15 // 15 minutes\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// components/HeavyChart.vue"],[2732,"To improve the readability of recursive rendering, extract a separate component for a node, which calls itself for the child elements. Use comprehensible names for the props, avoid deep nesting of the logic, apply a base case for stopping the recursion, and break complex nodes into subcomponents where necessary.","The problem of recursive rendering\\nWhen the data structure is nested — a tree of comments or a file system, for example — rendering it directly in one component becomes hard to read. The code turns into a cascade of nested conditions and maps, which complicates the maintenance.\\n\\nThe solution: the extraction of a recursive component\\nThe best way is to create a separate component for one node, which renders itself and calls itself for every child element. That isolates the node\'s logic and makes the structure obvious.\\n\\nSuch an approach lets you read the code easily: you can see that the node renders itself and its children, while the recursion is hidden inside the component.\\n\\nAdditional improvements\\n\\n- Use explicit names for the props — `node` rather than `item`, for example — so that it is clear at once that this is a node of a tree.\\n- Move the complex display logic (the icons, the statuses, for example) into separate components, so as not to overload the recursive component.\\n- Always check for the presence of child elements, in order to avoid infinite recursion.\\n- For very deep trees you can add a loading indicator or virtualization, but that is already an optimization.\\n\\nConclusion\\nRecursive rendering becomes readable if you extract a node component and use it for every level of the nesting. That makes understanding, testing and modifying the code easier, especially when the data structure is complex or changes often.","function TreeNode({ node }) {\\n  return (\\n    <li>\\n      <span>{node.name}</span>\\n      {node.children && (\\n        <ul>\\n          {node.children.map(child => (\\n            <TreeNode key={child.id} node={child} />\\n          ))}\\n        </ul>\\n      )}\\n    </li>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Tree({ data }) {\\n  return <ul><TreeNode node={data} /></ul>;\\n}"],[2733,"On the click the handler is called, which updates the state. React marks the component as changed and schedules a re-render. Rather than changing the DOM immediately, React creates a new virtual DOM, compares it with the old one and applies the minimal changes to the real DOM. That happens asynchronously, once the handler has finished.","How React handles a click and a change of state\\nWhen the user clicks on a button, the browser generates an event, which reaches the handler assigned through React. Inside the handler you call the state-update function (`setState` or `useState`, for example). React does not update the DOM instantly — it marks the component as \'dirty\' and puts a re-render task in the queue.\\n\\nOnce the current event handler has finished, React starts the re-render process. It creates a new virtual DOM, representing the desired state of the interface. React then compares the new virtual DOM with the previous one (that is called diffing) and computes the minimal set of changes that have to be made to the real DOM. Only those changes are applied to the browser\'s DOM, which makes the update efficient.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example, after the click `count` inside the handler stays the old value, because the update of the state is asynchronous. React will first finish running the function, then re-render the component with the new value of `count` and update the button\'s text in the DOM.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nSuch an approach lets React group several state updates into one re-render, avoiding redundant operations on the DOM. That is especially useful in large applications, where the state changes often. An understanding of this mechanism helps you avoid errors related to reading the state immediately after a call of the setter, and use functional updates properly.\\n\\nIn summary: on a click React defers the update of the DOM until the handler has finished, then performs the virtual diffing and applies only the changes needed. That makes the interface responsive and performant, so it matters to remember the asynchrony of the state when writing handlers.","function Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n\\n  function handleClick() {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // the update of the state\\n    console.log(\'count in the handler:\', count); // still 0\\n  }\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>;\\n}"],[2734,"Normally 0 is passed. That does not mean the code will run instantly: the browser puts the task in a queue and runs it once the current call stack has finished and the render has been updated. That trick lets you wait for the paint and then perform the action — measuring a DOM element\'s size, for example.","What a setTimeout with a zero delay is for\\nWhen you call `setTimeout(callback, 0)`, you do not guarantee an instant run. Instead the browser places the callback in the task queue, and it will run once the current synchronous code has finished and the browser has had the chance to update the interface. That is especially useful when an action has to be performed after React or another framework has finished the render and the browser has painted the changes.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe mechanism is based on the event loop. When the call stack is empty, the browser processes the task queue. The tasks from `setTimeout` go into the macrotask queue. The render happens between the processing of the tasks, so a callback with a zero delay normally runs once the browser has painted the current frame.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIn this example, without the `setTimeout` the measurement could return the old value, if the DOM had not yet repainted. With the zero delay we wait for the update.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\nSuch an approach is useful for measuring the DOM, for working with animations, for integration with libraries that require the element to be in the document already, or for running code after the render has finished in React components. You should not abuse it, however: if an action has to be performed after the render, it is better to use `useLayoutEffect` or `requestAnimationFrame`, depending on the task.\\n\\nIn summary: passing 0 into `setTimeout` is a simple, reliable way of deferring the execution until the next cycle of the event loop, which is often necessary for working with the DOM after a render.","import React, { useRef, useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const ref = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The render has finished, but the DOM may not yet have been updated\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      // Now the sizes can be measured safely\\n      console.log(ref.current.offsetWidth);\\n    }, 0);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div ref={ref}>The content</div>;\\n}"],[2735,"Defining a nested function that returns JSX inside a component is a bad practice. On every render of the parent a new component type is created, because of which React unmounts and mounts its whole subtree afresh, losing the state and causing redundant repaints. It is better to move such functions into separate top-level components, or to use useCallback/useMemo where necessary.","Why nested components are a problem\\nWhen you define a function that returns JSX inside another component\'s body, a new reference to that function is created every time the parent renders. React compares the types of the elements during the reconciliation. If the type has changed (and a new function is a new type), React considers that the old subtree is no longer needed, unmounts it completely and creates it afresh. That leads to the loss of the child elements\' state, the resetting of the focus, repeat requests and redundant work.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nHere, on every update of Parent the Child component will be recreated completely. If there is a useState or a useEffect inside Child, they will be reset. That is especially critical in large lists or with frequent updates.\\n\\nHow to do it properly\\nMove the nested functions into separate components at the top level of the module. Then the type is stable, and React can update only the parts that have changed efficiently.\\n\\nIf the function is needed only for computing JSX without any state of its own, you can use an ordinary function returning elements, but not as a component (with a lower-case letter), or wrap it in a useMemo, in order to avoid redundant computation.\\n\\nIn summary\\nAvoid defining components inside a render function. That makes the performance worse and leads to an unexpected loss of state. Always move your components to the top level, and for the helper functions use ordinary functions or hooks.","function Parent() {\\n  // Bad: every render creates a new type\\n  function Child() {\\n    return <div>Hello</div>;\\n  }\\n  return <Child />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  return <Child />;\\n}"],[2736,"In React the focus can be set by means of refs plus DOM methods such as focus(). For class components createRef() is used, and for functional ones useRef() and useEffect(). You can also use the autoFocus attribute for setting the focus initially. For the complex cases, callback refs or libraries such as react-focus-lock are applied.","An introduction to the management of the focus in React\\nThe management of the focus is an important part of creating accessible, interactive interfaces. In React the focus is not managed automatically, so the developer has to state explicitly which element should receive the focus. That matters especially for modal windows, forms and keyboard navigation.\\n\\nThe main ways of setting the focus\\nThe first and the simplest way is to use the `autoFocus` attribute. It works only when the component mounts and sets the focus on the element immediately after the render. It does not suit the dynamic management of the focus, however — when a modal window opens, for example.\\n\\nThe more flexible way is the use of refs. In class components a ref is created through `React.createRef()`, and in functional ones through `useRef()`. A ref lets you obtain access to the DOM element and call its `focus()` method.\\n\\nIn this example the focus is set after the component has mounted, thanks to the `useEffect` with an empty array of dependencies.\\n\\nThe dynamic management of the focus\\nTo set the focus in response to the user\'s actions — on a click on a button, for example — you can use event handlers. The ref is created in advance, while the focus is called at the moment needed.\\n\\nCallback refs and libraries\\nSometimes additional actions have to be performed when the ref is bound. For that, callback refs are used — functions that receive the DOM element or null. That lets you focus an element automatically when it appears, for example.\\n\\nFor complex scenarios, such as the management of the focus in modal windows or the prevention of the focus leaving, libraries exist — `react-focus-lock` or `focus-trap-react`, for example. They ensure the focus behaves properly in accordance with the accessibility requirements.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe management of the focus in React requires the explicit use of refs plus DOM methods. For the simple cases `autoFocus` or a `useRef` with a `useEffect` is enough. For the dynamic scenarios and for ensuring the accessibility, apply callback refs or specialized libraries. That improves the user experience and accords with the WCAG standards.","import React, { useRef, useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    inputRef.current.focus();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <input ref={inputRef} />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Form() {\\n  const nameRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    nameRef.current.focus();\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input ref={nameRef} />\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Focus input</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n<input ref={(node) => { if (node) node.focus(); }} />"],[2737,"styled-components let you write styles directly in the JS code, isolating them automatically and giving access to the props and the themes. That makes dynamic styles and the removal of unused code easier. They add a performance overhead, however, complicate SSR and require a new syntax to be learned. Ordinary CSS and CSS modules are simpler, faster and more familiar, but less flexible for dynamics.","An introduction to the approaches\\nOrdinary CSS is the classic way of styling, where the styles are held in separate files and applied globally. CSS modules solve the problem of the globality, by generating unique class names for every component automatically. styled-components is a CSS-in-JS library, which lets you write styles inside JavaScript files, creating React components with the styles already applied.\\n\\nThe advantages of styled-components\\n\\n- The isolation of the styles: every component receives a unique class, and conflicts are ruled out.\\n- Dynamic styles: you can change the styles on the basis of the props or the theme easily, without additional conditions in the JSX.\\n- The removal of dead code: the styles are bound to the component, so if the component is not used, the styles do not reach the bundle automatically.\\n- Theming: the built-in support for themes through a ThemeProvider makes the switching of the application\'s styles easier.\\n\\nThe disadvantages of styled-components\\n\\n- The performance: the generation of the styles on the fly adds work at runtime, which can slow the rendering of large lists down.\\n- The complexity of SSR: additional configuration is required for the server-side rendering, in order to avoid a flash of the styles.\\n- The barrier to entry: the developers have to know a new syntax and new concepts, which increases the learning time.\\n- The bundle size: the library itself adds weight to the final JS file.\\n\\nThe comparison with ordinary CSS and CSS modules\\nOrdinary CSS is simple and fast, but it suffers from the global scope and from the difficulty of maintaining large projects. CSS modules solve the problem of the isolation while preserving the familiar syntax, but they do not give the same flexibility for dynamic styles as styled-components do. To change a button\'s colour depending on a prop, for example, in CSS modules you would have to use conditional classes, while in styled-components simply interpolation:\\n\\nThe practical application\\nstyled-components are especially useful in projects that have a lot of dynamic interfaces, that require theming, or where the team already uses React and wants to write the styles in the same file. For simple static sites or for projects with strict performance requirements, however, it is better to choose ordinary CSS or CSS modules.\\n\\nConclusion\\nstyled-components are a powerful tool for modern React development, but they are worth applying deliberately, with the requirements on the speed and the project\'s complexity in mind. For most applications with moderate dynamics, CSS modules can be a simpler, more effective solution, whereas styled-components are justified with the active use of themes and props in the styles.",null],[2738,"On a change of the state React starts a repeat render of the component. It compares the virtual DOM trees and updates only the parts of the real DOM that have changed. If the new element appears without a suitable key or does not match in its structure, React may recreate the DOM node, which leads to the loss of the focus. To preserve the focus, you have to use stable keys and avoid the recreation of the elements.","What happens on a change of the state\\nWhen setState or a useState setter is called, React marks the component as requiring an update and schedules a repeat render. Rather than changing the DOM immediately, React creates a new virtual tree of elements, describing the desired interface. Then the reconciliation process happens: React compares the new virtual tree with the previous one and computes the minimal changes for the real DOM.\\n\\nAt the comparison stage React uses heuristics: if the element\'s type (a div or an input, for example) matches, it updates only the properties that have changed; if the type differs, the old node is deleted and a new one is created. The key point is the presence of the key attribute. If an element\'s key changes or is absent, React may recreate the node, even if the structure is similar.\\n\\nWhy the focus is lost\\nThe focus is a state of the browser, bound to a particular DOM node. When React deletes a node and creates a new one (because of a change of the key or the type, for example), the browser loses the reference to the old node and the focus is reset. Even if the new element looks identical, it does not receive the focus automatically, because the browser does not know that it has to be set.\\n\\nLet us look at an example: a list of inputs where, on the addition of a new element at the start of the list, React recreates all of the elements if the key is not set or is set by index. As a result the user loses the focus on the input field they are in.\\n\\nHere key={index} leads to all of the inputs receiving new keys when an element is added at the start (0,1,2 instead of 0,1), so React recreates every node. The focus is lost. The right solution is to use a stable unique identifier:\\n\\nHow to preserve the focus\\n\\n- Always set a stable key for the elements of a list, based on a unique identifier from the data rather than on the index.\\n- Avoid changing the component\'s structure unnecessarily — do not change an element\'s type from a div to a span on an update, for example.\\n- If the focus has to be set programmatically after the render, use useRef and a useEffect with a dependency on the state.\\n\\nIn the cases where an element appears conditionally, you can use a ref and call focus() in an effect. That guarantees the focus will be set once the new node has mounted.\\n\\nConclusion: the loss of the focus in React is a consequence of the recreation of the DOM nodes. Apply stable keys and minimize the changes of the structure, in order to preserve the user\'s input and the focus. If the recreation is unavoidable, manage the focus explicitly through refs and effects.","function App() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([\'a\', \'b\']);\\n  const addItem = () => setItems([\'new\', ...items]);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map((item, index) => (\\n        <input key={index} defaultValue={item} />\\n      ))}\\n      <button onClick={addItem}>Add</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst [items, setItems] = useState([{id: 1, value: \'a\'}, {id: 2, value: \'b\'}]);\\n// key={item.id} — then React will update only the new elements, while the existing ones stay untouched"],[2739,"An alert blocks the running of the code and looks ugly, while drawing it in the UI lets you control the style and does not interrupt the user\'s work. For modern applications it is better to use custom notification components — a toast or inline messages, for example. An alert suits only simple scripts or debugging.","Why an alert is not the best choice\\nThe native `alert()` is a function built into the browser, which shows a modal window with some text and an OK button. Its main drawback is that it blocks the running of the JavaScript and the user\'s interaction with the page until the window is closed. That creates a sharp break in the user experience, especially if there are several messages in a row. Besides, an alert\'s appearance cannot be styled; it looks the same in every browser and does not match the application\'s design.\\n\\nThe advantages of drawing it in the UI\\nWhen you show a message through DOM elements, you control the appearance, the animations, the placement and the behaviour completely. The user can keep working while the notification appears and disappears. That matters especially for an SPA, where the smoothness of the interface is critical. You can create a toast component, for example, which disappears automatically after a few seconds, or an inline message next to a form, which highlights the error in a particular field.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation of a toast notification\\n\\nIn real projects in React or Vue, such notifications are normally moved into a separate component and managed through the state or through a library (react-toastify, for example).\\n\\nWhen an alert is nevertheless permissible\\nIn simple scripts that have no user interface, or when debugging code, an alert can be a fast way of checking a variable\'s value. For production applications, however, it is better to avoid it entirely.\\n\\nIn summary\\nUse drawing in the UI for all of the user\'s notifications — that gives flexibility, improves the perception and does not get in the way of the work. Leave the alert only for console experiments or temporary debugging.","// A simple example in plain JS\\nfunction showToast(message, type = \'success\') {\\n  const toast = document.createElement(\'div\');\\n  toast.className = `toast toast-${type}`;\\n  toast.textContent = message;\\n  document.body.appendChild(toast);\\n  setTimeout(() => toast.remove(), 3000);\\n}\\n\\n// The usage\\nshowToast(\'The data has been saved\');\\nshowToast(\'A network error\', \'error\');"],[2740,"To avoid cascading re-rendering, use React.memo for memoizing the child components, pass stable references to the functions through useCallback, and memoize the heavy computations with useMemo. You can also move the changing state into separate components, or use a context broken into several providers.","The problem of cascading re-rendering\\nIn React, when the state changes in the parent component, all of its child components repaint by default, even if their props have not changed. That can cause cascading updates and reduce the performance, especially in large trees of components.\\n\\nThe main tools of optimization\\nThe first step is to wrap the child components in `React.memo`. That makes React compare the previous and the new props (shallowly) and skip the render if they are equal. memo works only if the references in the props are stable, however. The handler functions passed downwards therefore have to be wrapped in `useCallback`, and the objects and arrays in `useMemo`.\\n\\nIn the example `Child` will not re-render when `count` changes, if `data` and `onClick` do not change. Here `data` depends on `count`, however, so when the counter changes the child component will update anyway — that is expected.\\n\\nAdditional approaches\\n\\n- Move the frequently changing state into a separate wrapper component, so that its descendants are not affected.\\n- Use `useContext` with a separation into several contexts, so that the components subscribe only to the parts of the data they need.\\n- For lists, use stable keys and the memoization of the list\'s items.\\n\\nConclusion\\nApply the memoization deliberately: measure the performance first, then optimize only the problem areas. React.memo, useCallback and useMemo are the standard tools for controlling the re-renders, but their excessive use can complicate the code without any real benefit.","const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, data }) => {\\n  console.log(\'Child render\');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>{data.value}</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => setCount(c => c + 1), []);\\n  const data = useMemo(() => ({ value: count }), [count]);\\n  return <Child onClick={handleClick} data={data} />;\\n}"],[2741,"In React Native Web the styles are set through JavaScript objects, by means of StyleSheet.create, rather than through CSS files. Only the properties supported by the mobile components are used — flexbox for the layout, for example. There is no support for CSS selectors, pseudo-classes or media queries directly. Instead of classes, style props are applied, while the responsiveness is achieved through JS logic or through libraries.","The main differences\\nReact Native Web lets you use one and the same code for the mobile and the web platforms, but the styling differs from the traditional web. Rather than CSS files and selectors, you work with style objects, which are passed into the components\' style prop. That is closer to inline styles, but with an optimization through StyleSheet.create.\\n\\nThe key difference is the absence of the cascade and of inheritance. Every component receives its styles explicitly; there are no global rules or specificity. There is also no support for pseudo-classes (hover, active) or for media queries in the classic form. For the responsiveness you have to use JS conditions or libraries such as react-native-web-responsive.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHere you can see that the styles are ordinary objects rather than CSS rules. Properties such as flex, justifyContent and alignItems work as in the mobile version, but on the web they are translated into CSS flexbox.\\n\\nThe application\\nSuch an approach is convenient for cross-platform development, when both mobile applications and the web have to be supported. For complex web interfaces with rich animation or with complex responsive layouts, however, it can be limited. In such cases it is better to use ordinary CSS or CSS-in-JS libraries, which give more capabilities.\\n\\nIn summary: React Native Web suits projects where the unification of the code between the platforms matters, but it requires you to give the familiar CSS capabilities up in favour of a limited set of styles.","import { StyleSheet, Text, View } from \'react-native\';\\n\\nconst styles = StyleSheet.create({\\n  container: {\\n    flex: 1,\\n    justifyContent: \'center\',\\n    alignItems: \'center\',\\n    backgroundColor: \'#f0f0f0\',\\n  },\\n  title: {\\n    fontSize: 20,\\n    fontWeight: \'bold\',\\n    color: \'#333\',\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nexport default function App() {\\n  return (\\n    <View style={styles.container}>\\n      <Text style={styles.title}>Hello React Native Web</Text>\\n    </View>\\n  );\\n}"],[2742,"The Open/Closed principle means that classes and modules should be open for extension but closed for modification. On the frontend that is achieved through composition — render props, for example: a component takes a function, which determines how the content is to be drawn, letting you extend the behaviour without changing the source code. That improves the reusability and reduces the risk of regressions.","What is the Open/Closed principle?\\nThe Open/Closed principle (OCP) is one of the five SOLID principles. It states: software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension but closed for modification. That means you can add new functionality without touching the code that has already been written and tested. Otherwise every change can break the existing behaviour.\\n\\nHow does it work on the frontend?\\nIn frontend development, especially in React, this principle is often implemented through the composition of the components. One of the patterns is render props. The essence is that a component takes a function (a render prop), which returns React elements. The component manages the logic and the state, while the outside code decides how exactly the data is to be drawn. Thus the component stays closed for modification but open for extension: you can use it with different render functions without editing the component itself.\\n\\nAn example in React\\nImagine a `MouseTracker` component, which tracks the cursor\'s position. Rather than hard-wiring the output, it uses a render prop:\\n\\nNow, if the coordinates have to be shown in another format or styles have to be added, you simply pass in a different render function. The `MouseTracker` component does not change — it is closed for modification but open for extension.\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\nRender props are widely used in libraries (React Router, Formik, for example) and in your own components, when the logic has to be separated from the presentation. That also helps you avoid prop drilling and makes the code more declarative.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Open/Closed principle through render props lets you create flexible, reusable components, which can be adapted to new requirements easily without the risk of breaking the existing functionality. Apply this approach when you have repeated logic but different ways of displaying the data.","class MouseTracker extends React.Component {\\n  state = { x: 0, y: 0 };\\n\\n  handleMouseMove = (event) => {\\n    this.setState({ x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY });\\n  };\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return (\\n      <div onMouseMove={this.handleMouseMove}>\\n        {this.props.render(this.state)}\\n      </div>\\n    );\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The usage: we extend the behaviour without changing MouseTracker\\n<MouseTracker render={({ x, y }) => (\\n  <h1>The coordinates: {x}, {y}</h1>\\n)} />"],[2743,"Yes, Apollo GraphQL can be used for the local management of state. For that, client-only fields are applied, which are added to the schema through the @client directive, plus reactive variables, which let you hold and update local data. Apollo Client synchronizes that data with the cache automatically, which makes managing the state easier without additional libraries.","An introduction to Apollo\'s local state\\nApollo GraphQL was originally created for working with remote data, but its capabilities go far beyond simple requests to a server. Starting with version 3, Apollo Client provides a full mechanism for managing local state, which integrates with the cache and lets you handle the data in the same way as the server\'s. That is especially useful when UI state, the user\'s settings or temporary data has to be held without addressing the backend.\\n\\nClient-only fields and reactive variables\\nThe basis of the local management is the client-only fields. They are declared in the GraphQL queries with the @client directive and are not sent to the server. You can add an isLoggedIn field to the User type, for example, which will be held only in Apollo\'s cache. To work with such fields, reactive variables are used — special objects, which update all of the components subscribed to them automatically.\\n\\nSuch an approach lets you avoid superfluous libraries such as Redux or MobX, since Apollo already manages the cache and the reactivity. Client-only fields can be combined with server data in one query, which makes the application\'s architecture simpler.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nApollo\'s local state is especially useful in medium and large React applications where GraphQL is already used. You can hold a shopping cart, the search filters or the modal windows, for example. Apollo caches the results automatically and ensures the consistency of the data between the components.\\n\\nIn this example isLoggedIn does not exist on the server, but Apollo will return it from the local cache. For that you have to configure the field in the cache through typePolicies, or use reactive variables.\\n\\nConclusion\\nApollo GraphQL is not only a client for server requests but also a powerful tool for managing local state. If your application already uses Apollo, it is worth considering it for holding UI state, in order to reduce the number of dependencies and make the synchronization of the data easier. For very complex state with a great deal of business logic, however, a specialized solution such as Redux Toolkit may be more convenient.","import { makeVar, useReactiveVar } from \'@apollo/client\';\\n\\n// We create a reactive variable\\nexport const isLoggedInVar = makeVar(false);\\n\\n// We use it in a component\\nfunction App() {\\n  const isLoggedIn = useReactiveVar(isLoggedInVar);\\n  return <div>{isLoggedIn ? \'Welcome\' : \'Please log in\'}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// We update the value\\nisLoggedInVar(true);\\n\\nconst GET_USER = gql`\\n  query GetUser {\\n    user {\\n      id\\n      name\\n      isLoggedIn @client\\n    }\\n  }\\n`;"],[2744,"Concurrent rendering is React\'s ability to interrupt and restart the rendering of components, so as not to block the main thread. React 18 switches it on by default, letting it break the work into parts and prioritize the updates. That is needed for a smooth UI during frequent updates — when text is typed or data loads, for example.","What is concurrent rendering?\\nConcurrent rendering is an approach in React in which the rendering of the components can be interrupted, paused and resumed later. Rather than performing the whole rendering synchronously and blocking the main thread, React breaks the work into small parts and performs them in the background, giving priority to urgent tasks such as the user\'s input.\\n\\nThat became possible thanks to the new algorithm presented in React 18. Previously the rendering was synchronous: once it had begun, it could not be stopped. Now React can \\"look\\" into the future and decide which updates are more important and which can be deferred.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nReact uses the concept of time slicing. It breaks the rendering into small pieces and between them checks whether there are more urgent tasks. If the user interacts with the interface, React interrupts the current rendering, handles the event, and then continues from the place where it stopped.\\n\\nTo manage the priorities, the `useTransition` and `useDeferredValue` hooks are used. `useTransition`, for example, lets you mark an update as \'not urgent\', so that React can defer its performance without blocking the input.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nConcurrent rendering is useful in applications with a large number of state updates — in code editors, in dashboards with charts or in chats, for example. It helps you avoid the interface \'hanging\' while heavy computations are performed, making the interaction more responsive.\\n\\nIt matters to understand that concurrent rendering does not make the code faster; it only distributes the load over time, improving the perception of the performance.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse concurrent rendering when the responsiveness of the interface has to be preserved during frequent, heavy updates. That is especially relevant for complex applications, where synchronous rendering can cause delays and make the user experience worse.","import { useTransition, useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(\'\');\\n  const [list, setList] = useState([]);\\n\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    setValue(e.target.value);\\n    startTransition(() => {\\n      // A heavy operation — the filtering of a large list, for example\\n      setList(filterItems(e.target.value));\\n    });\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={value} onChange={handleChange} />\\n      {isPending ? \'Updating...\' : <List items={list} />}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2745,"Sorting an array from the props directly is a bad practice. The sort() method mutates the original array, which can change the parent component\'s data and cause unpredictable behaviour. You have to create a copy of the array before the sorting — through the spread operator or slice(), for example.","The problem of mutating the props\\nIn React the props are immutable by convention. When you call sort() on an array passed in through the props, you change the very object that belongs to the parent component. That breaks the unidirectional data flow and can lead to the parent component displaying data that is already sorted without an update of the state, which will cause bugs and difficulties in debugging.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nRather than mutating the original array, you should create a copy of it and sort the copy. That guarantees the original data will stay untouched while the component works with its own representation of the data.\\n\\nWhen to use it\\nIf the sorting is needed only for the display, it is better to perform it at the moment of the rendering, by means of a useMemo, in order to avoid redundant computation on every render. If the sorting has to affect the state, then a callback has to be passed upwards, so that the parent updates the data.\\n\\nConclusion: always work with copies of the data obtained through the props, in order to preserve the predictability and the purity of the components. That matters especially in large applications, where a mutation of the props can lead to hard-to-catch bugs.","// Wrong: a mutation of the props\\nconst sorted = props.items.sort((a, b) => a - b);\\n\\n// Right: the creation of a copy before the sorting\\nconst sorted = [...props.items].sort((a, b) => a - b);\\n// or\\nconst sorted = props.items.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);"],[2746,"The key attribute helps React identify every element of a list during the updates. Without a stable key React can match the elements before and after the change wrongly, which leads to errors in the state, the order or the input. Use unique, stable keys — an id from a database, for example — rather than the array index.","What key in React is for\\nWhen you render a list of elements in React, every element has to have a unique key attribute. That attribute lets React track which elements have been changed, added or removed. Without a key React is forced to recreate every element of the list on every update, which can lead to the loss of state (the text typed in a field, for example) and to the order being displayed wrongly.\\n\\nHow the matching of the elements works\\nReact uses the reconciliation algorithm, which compares the virtual DOM with the previous state. The keys help the algorithm determine quickly which elements have stayed, which have been added and which have been removed. If the keys are stable, React can update only the parts that have changed efficiently.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example every element of the list has a unique id. If you add a new element at the start of the array, React will be able to match the existing elements properly and will not recreate them, preserving their state.\\n\\nWhy the array index should not be used as the key\\nThe use of the index as the key can cause problems when elements are added or removed from the middle of a list. If you remove the first element, for example, all of the following elements will receive new indexes, and React may match them wrongly, which will lead to errors in the state or the order.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways use stable unique keys for the elements of a list, such as identifiers from a database or other constant values. That will ensure the interface updates properly and will avoid problems with the state and the order of the display.","function TodoList({ todos }) {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {todos.map((todo) => (\\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.text}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2747,"When subscribing to events or starting timers in a component, you must be sure to clean them up when the component unmounts. Lifecycle methods are used for that, such as componentWillUnmount in class components, or a useEffect with a clean-up function in functional ones. Otherwise the subscriptions and the timers keep working, referring to a component that has been removed, which leads to memory leaks.","The problem of memory leaks\\nWhen a component subscribes to global events (scroll on window, for example) or starts timers (setInterval, setTimeout), those operations create references to functions that can address the component\'s state or DOM. If the component is removed from the tree but the subscription or the timer has not been cleaned up, they keep existing in memory, holding on to the component and its dependencies. That leads to memory leaks, to reduced performance and to possible errors when an attempt is made to update the state of an unmounted component.\\n\\nThe solution in class components\\nIn class components the componentWillUnmount method is used for the clean-up. In it you have to remove all of the subscriptions and stop the timers.\\n\\nThe solution in functional components\\nIn functional components the useEffect hook is used. If you return a function from the useEffect, it will be called when the component unmounts (or before the effect is started again).\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Always clean your subscriptions and timers up when the component unmounts.\\n- Use an empty array of dependencies in the useEffect, if the subscription is created once.\\n- If the subscription depends on props or state, add them to the array of dependencies, so that the effect is recreated on the changes.\\n- For the events that are added to the component\'s own elements (onClick, for example), no clean-up is required, since React manages them automatically.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe proper clean-up of the resources is an obligatory practice when working with global events and timers. It prevents memory leaks and makes the application stable and performant. Always use the clean-up mechanisms React provides, in order to avoid unexpected behaviour.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    window.addEventListener(\'scroll\', this.handleScroll);\\n    this.timer = setInterval(this.tick, 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  componentWillUnmount() {\\n    window.removeEventListener(\'scroll\', this.handleScroll);\\n    clearInterval(this.timer);\\n  }\\n\\n  handleScroll = () => { /* ... */ };\\n  tick = () => { /* ... */ };\\n}\\n\\nimport { useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleScroll = () => { /* ... */ };\\n    window.addEventListener(\'scroll\', handleScroll);\\n    const timer = setInterval(() => { /* ... */ }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener(\'scroll\', handleScroll);\\n      clearInterval(timer);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // an empty array of dependencies — the effect will run once\\n}"],[2748,"React is a library for creating user interfaces, which uses a component-based approach plus a virtual DOM. Unlike vanilla JavaScript, where you manipulate the DOM directly, React updates only the parts of the page that have changed automatically. That makes the development of complex applications easier and improves the performance.","The main difference\\nVanilla JavaScript is a programming language, which works with the DOM (the Document Object Model) directly. You find the elements yourself, change their content, add event handlers and manage the application\'s state by hand. React is a library built on JavaScript, which offers a different approach: you describe what the interface should look like depending on the data, and React itself takes care of updating the DOM.\\n\\nThe component-based approach\\nIn React the interface is broken into reusable components. Every component is a function or a class, which returns JSX (an extension of JavaScript, similar to HTML). That lets you isolate the logic and the styles, making the testing and the maintenance of the code easier. In vanilla JavaScript you often write long scripts, which mix the markup and the logic.\\n\\nThe virtual DOM\\nReact uses a virtual DOM — a lightweight copy of the real DOM. When a component\'s state changes, React creates a new virtual DOM, compares it with the previous one and computes the minimal changes. It then applies those changes to the real DOM. That is considerably faster than repainting the whole page, as often has to be done in vanilla JavaScript.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nLet us compare a simple counter. In vanilla JavaScript:\\n\\nIn React:\\n\\nIn React you do not touch the DOM directly — you change the state, and the interface updates automatically.\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nVanilla JavaScript suits simple pages or small widgets, where complex state is not required. React is justified for large applications with a great many interactions, where the reusability of the components and the predictability of the updates matter.\\n\\nIn summary: React is a tool that abstracts the manual work with the DOM away and helps you structure the code. If you are creating a scalable application with a dynamic interface, React will save time and reduce the number of bugs.","let count = 0;\\nconst button = document.getElementById(\'btn\');\\nconst display = document.getElementById(\'count\');\\nbutton.addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n  count++;\\n  display.textContent = count;\\n});\\n\\nimport { useState } from \'react\';\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>{count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+1</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2749,"single-spa uses an import map for determining where every application\'s JavaScript modules are, and SystemJS as the module loader, which works with the import map in the browser. That lets it load and unload the applications dynamically without reloading the page.","How single-spa loads the applications\\nsingle-spa is a framework for a microservice architecture on the frontend. It lets you run several independent applications on one page. The key question is how those applications are loaded into the browser. The answer: through the combination of an import map and SystemJS.\\n\\nAn import map is a browser standard, which determines how module names are resolved on an import. In single-spa the import map sets the correspondence between an application\'s name (\'app1\', for example) and the URL of its JavaScript file. SystemJS is a module loader, which can work with an import map and supports the dynamic loading of modules in the browser, even if the browser does not support import maps natively.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example the import map states that the \'app1\' module is at the address localhost:3001. SystemJS reads that map and loads the file needed. single-spa uses SystemJS for loading the root configuration file and all of the registered applications.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nWithout SystemJS and the import map, single-spa would not be able to load the applications dynamically on demand. That lets you deploy the applications independently: a team can update app1 without touching app2. It also makes the management of the versions and of the dependencies between the microservices easier.\\n\\nConclusion: single-spa uses an import map for describing the modules available and SystemJS for loading them in the browser. That is the standard approach in a microservice architecture on the frontend, which provides flexibility and scalability.","// index.html\\n<script type=\\"systemjs-importmap\\">\\n  {\\n    \\"imports\\": {\\n      \\"app1\\": \\"http://localhost:3001/app1.js\\",\\n      \\"app2\\": \\"http://localhost:3002/app2.js\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n<\/script>\\n<script src=\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/systemjs/dist/system.min.js\\"><\/script>\\n<script>\\n  System.import(\'app1\'); // it loads app1.js\\n<\/script>"],[2750,"One-way binding is a flow of data only from the model to the view (or the other way round), which makes the state predictable. Two-way binding synchronizes the model and the view in both directions automatically, making the code simpler but the debugging harder. React uses a one-way flow, Angular a two-way one through ngModel.","The basic idea\\nData binding determines how an application\'s state (the model) is synchronized with the interface (the view). In the one-way mode the changes happen only in one direction: either the model updates the UI or the UI changes the model, but not both automatically at once. In the two-way mode any change in the model is reflected in the UI instantly, and vice versa — the user\'s input changes the model at once.\\n\\nOne-way binding\\nThis approach is used in React and Redux. The state is held in one place, and the UI is simply a function of the state. When the user enters data, you call a handler explicitly, which updates the state, and React then repaints the component. That makes the data flow predictable and makes debugging easier, since it is always clear where a change came from.\\n\\nTwo-way binding\\nIn Angular the ngModel directive is used, which binds a property of the model and an input element automatically. That cuts the amount of code down, but with complex logic it can lead to implicit changes of the state, which makes tracking the errors harder.\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\nOne-way binding is better for large applications, where predictability and testability matter. Two-way binding is convenient for simple forms and prototypes, where the speed of the development matters more than strict control. Many frameworks let you combine both approaches.\\n\\nIn summary: one-way binding gives control and clarity, two-way binding convenience and brevity. The choice depends on the project\'s scale and on the requirements on the reliability.","// React: the one-way flow\\nfunction Input() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(\'\');\\n  return <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />;\\n}\\n\\n// Angular: the two-way flow\\n<input [(ngModel)]=\\"username\\" />\\n// username is updated automatically on the input"],[2751,"React Router is a library for adding routing to React applications, letting the user move between pages without a full reload.","React Router is a library that adds routing to React applications, providing smooth transitions between components. It lets developers build multi-page applications with dynamic content. React Router\'s core components, such as `<BrowserRouter>` and `<Route>`, control routes and decide which components render for the current URL. The tool improves the user experience, making interaction with a web application feel natural and similar to working with an ordinary website. A practical example is a profile page whose route depends on the user ID in the URL.","import { BrowserRouter as Router, Route, Switch } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nfunction App() {\\nreturn (\\n  <Router>\\n    <Switch>\\n      <Route path=\\"/profile/:id\\" component={ProfilePage} />\\n      {/* Other routes */}\\n    </Switch>\\n  </Router>\\n);\\n}\\nfunction ProfilePage({ match }) {\\n  const userId = match.params.id;\\n  return <div>Profile of the user with ID: {userId}</div>;\\n}"],[2752,"BrowserRouter and HashRouter are two routing methods in React Router. BrowserRouter uses real URLs, while HashRouter uses hash fragments (#).","BrowserRouter and HashRouter are two ways to add routing to React applications. `BrowserRouter` uses real URLs, which means addresses can be changed in the browser\'s address bar and support SEO. For example, the URL `https://example.com/profile/1` will be correctly interpreted as the profile of the user with ID 1. `HashRouter`, by contrast, uses hash fragments (#) to change the URL, which allows the route to be manipulated without really changing the URL itself. It is less preferable because it does not support SEO and can be limiting when integrating with a server. The choice depends on the project\'s specific requirements, such as SEO support and server interaction.",null],[2753,"The history object is accessed in React Router through `useHistory()` or `useNavigate()` in function components, which lets you manipulate the current route.","In React Router, access to the history object is provided through `useHistory()` or `useNavigate()`.\\n`useHistory()` gives access to the history object, which lets you change the current route — for example with `push()` to add a new route, or `goBack()` to return to the previous page.\\n`useNavigate()` works similarly but returns a function for manipulating the route. These tools let you build dynamic, user-oriented applications that provide smooth and intuitive interaction.\\nAn example is a \\"Back\\" button on a profile page that uses `goBack()` to return to the previous page.\\n\\n`useNavigate()` works similarly but returns a function for changing the route programmatically:","import { useHistory } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nfunction SomeComponent() {\\n  const history = useHistory();\\n  const navigateToProfile = () => {\\n    history.push(\'/profile/1\');\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={navigateToProfile}>Go to profile</button>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useNavigate } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nfunction SomeComponent() {\\n  const navigate = useNavigate();\\n  const navigateToProfile = () => {\\n    navigate(\'/profile/1\');\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={navigateToProfile}>Go to profile</button>;\\n}"],[2754,"React Router DOM provides key components such as `<BrowserRouter>`, `<Routes>`, `<Route>` and `<Link>`. `<BrowserRouter>` wraps the application to enable routing. `<Routes>` holds a set of routes, and `<Route>` determines which component renders when a particular URL matches. `<Link>` creates links that allow navigation without reloading the page.","React Router DOM is a library for managing routing in React. Its core components:\\n\\n- `<BrowserRouter>`: The main wrapper that connects routing to the browser history. All routes must live inside this component.\\n\\n- `<Routes>`: A container for routes. It replaces `<Switch>` from older versions and defines the set of routes to check.\\n\\n- `<Route>`: Defines a path (`path`) and the associated component (`element`). If the current URL matches `path`, the given component renders.\\n\\n- `<Link>`: Replaces standard HTML links (`<a>`) for navigating between pages without a reload.\\n\\nThese components are used in most applications to build an SPA (Single Page Application), where moving between pages happens without a full page reload.","import { BrowserRouter } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <BrowserRouter>\\n    {/* Routes go here */}\\n  </BrowserRouter>\\n);\\n\\nimport { Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst AppRoutes = () => (\\n  <Routes>\\n    <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n    <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n  </Routes>\\n);\\n\\nimport { Link } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Navbar = () => (\\n  <nav>\\n    <Link to=\\"/\\">Home</Link>\\n    <Link to=\\"/about\\">About</Link>\\n  </nav>\\n);"],[2755,"`<Route>` defines a single route and is tied to a specific component that renders when the URL matches. `<Routes>` is a container for `<Route>` and is responsible for finding the first matching route. Without `<Routes>`, `<Route>` components will not work correctly in newer versions of React Router.","In React Router DOM, `<Route>` and `<Routes>` do different jobs:\\n`<Route>`: This component describes a route: the path (`path`) and which component (`element`) renders on a match. It is the basic building block of routing.\\n\\n`<Routes>`: This is the container for all `<Route>` elements. In React Router 6 and above it replaces `<Switch>`. `<Routes>` checks routes top to bottom and renders the first one that matches. Without `<Routes>`, routes will not work.\\n\\nThe key difference:\\n\\n- `<Route>` defines a single route.\\n\\n- `<Routes>` organises checking them, in order to pick the right route for the current URL.","<Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n\\n<Routes>\\n  <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n  <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n</Routes>"],[2756,"If two routes match, `<Routes>` picks the first one that fully matches the current URL. This is called an \\"exact match\\". You manage it by ordering routes from most specific to least specific.","React Router DOM handles routes on a \\"first match\\" basis. This means `<Routes>` checks each `<Route>` top to bottom and picks the first whose `path` fully matches the current URL.\\nFor example:\\n\\nIf the current URL is `/about/team`, `<Team />` renders, because that route is more specific.\\nRoute ordering:\\nSpecific routes go higher up.\\nUsing wildcards (`*`):\\nTo specify more general routes.\\n\\nThis approach gives you control over routing behaviour and prevents conflicts.","<Routes>\\n  <Route path=\\"/about/team\\" element={<Team />} />\\n  <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n</Routes>\\n\\n<Route path=\\"*\\" element={<NotFound />} />"],[2757,"`<Link>` from React Router is used for navigation inside a React application, preventing a full page reload. Unlike `<a>`, which refreshes the whole page, `<Link>` updates only the components that need it and preserves application state.","In React applications, `<Link>` replaces the standard `<a>` tag for internal navigation. The main differences:\\nPage reload:\\n\\n- `<a>` triggers a full page reload, because the browser goes to the server.\\n\\n- `<Link>` lets React Router intercept the transition and change only the content, which makes navigation instant.\\n\\nPreserving state:\\n\\n- With `<a>`, React state (for example data inside components) is reset.\\n\\n- `<Link>` preserves application state, because it works without a reload.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nIn short, `<Link>` is used to create smooth, fast navigation inside a Single Page Application (SPA).","import { Link } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Navigation = () => (\\n  <nav>\\n    <Link to=\\"/\\">Home</Link>\\n    <Link to=\\"/about\\">About</Link>\\n  </nav>\\n);"],[2758,"A dynamic route segment is a part of the path written as `:paramName`, and it is required. Query parameters are passed in the string after `?` and are optional. Dynamic segments are part of the URL structure, while query parameters suit passing extra information.","Dynamic route segments:\\n\\n- Are written in the route path using a colon (`:`).\\n\\n- Are a required part of the URL.\\n\\n- Are used to identify specific resources (for example a user ID).\\n\\nExample URL: `/user/123`. Here `123` is the dynamic segment `userId`.\\nQuery parameters:\\n\\n- Are passed after `?` in the URL in `key=value` form.\\n\\n- Are not required and can be added to any route.\\n\\n- Are used to pass extra data, for example sorting or filters.\\n\\nThe key difference:\\n\\n- Dynamic segments are part of the path, included in the route definition.\\n\\n- Query parameters are extra information that does not affect the route.","<Route path=\\"/user/:userId\\" element={<User />} />\\n\\n/products?sort=price&order=asc"],[2759,"Route parameters can be read with the `useParams` hook, which returns an object whose keys match the dynamic segments. Query parameters can be extracted via `useSearchParams`, which gives access to the query string.","React Router provides convenient hooks for working with route parameters:\\nFor dynamic route segments: use the `useParams` hook:\\n\\nIf the route is `/user/:userId` and the URL is `/user/123`, then `userId` will be `123`.\\nFor query parameters: use the `useSearchParams` hook:\\n\\nFor the URL `/products?sort=price`, `sort` will be `price`.\\nThese hooks let you extract parameters efficiently and use them to render components or run logic.","import { useParams } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst User = () => {\\n  const { userId } = useParams(); // Read the value of the userId segment\\n  return <div>User ID: {userId}</div>;\\n};\\n\\nimport { useSearchParams } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Products = () => {\\n  const [searchParams] = useSearchParams();\\n  const sort = searchParams.get(\'sort\'); // Read the value of the sort parameter\\n  return <div>Sort by: {sort}</div>;\\n};"],[2760,"`useLocation` is a React Router hook that returns an object with information about the current URL. It provides data such as the path (`pathname`), the query string (`search`) and the route state (`state`), if one was passed.","The `useLocation` hook gives access to the current location in the application.\\nThe returned object has the following properties:\\n\\n- `pathname`: A string representing the current path.\\n\\n- `search`: The query string, for example `?sort=price`.\\n\\n- `hash`: The URL fragment after `#`, for example `#section1`.\\n\\n- `state`: Extra data passed during navigation, for example through `Link` or `navigate`.\\n\\nExample usage:\\n\\n`useLocation` is often used to check the current path, render matching content, or work with passed data.","import { useLocation } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst CurrentLocation = () => {\\n  const location = useLocation();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Pathname: {location.pathname}</p>\\n      <p>Search: {location.search}</p>\\n      <p>Hash: {location.hash}</p>\\n      <p>State: {JSON.stringify(location.state)}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2761,"To create nested routes in React Router you use a route structure where one `<Route>` contains another. Child routes render inside the parent component using the `<Outlet>` component.","Nested routes let you organise components into a hierarchy where child routes render inside the parent. This is achieved using the `<Outlet>` component to render the child routes.\\nExample:\\n\\n- The parent component (`Layout`) holds shared content, for example a header and a footer.\\n\\n- `<Outlet>` marks the place where child routes (`/about`, `/contact`) will render.\\n\\nWhen navigating to `/about`, `<About />` renders inside `<Layout>`.","import { Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Routes>\\n    <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Layout />}>\\n      <Route path=\\"about\\" element={<About />} />\\n      <Route path=\\"contact\\" element={<Contact />} />\\n    </Route>\\n  </Routes>\\n);\\nconst Layout = () => (\\n  <div>\\n    <header>Header</header>\\n    <Outlet /> {/* Child routes render here */}\\n    <footer>Footer</footer>\\n  </div>\\n);"],[2762,"`Outlet` is a React Router component that renders a child route inside the parent component. It is used when routes are nested, to render child route content in a specified place.","`Outlet` is the \\"empty slot\\" in a parent route\'s component where child routes render. It lets you build a hierarchical route structure.\\nExample usage:\\n\\n- Defining the routes:\\n\\n- The parent component with `Outlet`:\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- When navigating to `/dashboard`, `<Header>`, `<Dashboard />` and `<Footer>` render.\\n\\n- When navigating to `/settings`, `<Settings />` renders instead of `<Dashboard />`.\\n\\nWhen it is used:\\n\\n- With nested routes, when you need to add a shared shell (for example a header or footer).\\n\\n- To render dynamic content depending on the current child route.","<Routes>\\n  <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Layout />}>\\n    <Route path=\\"dashboard\\" element={<Dashboard />} />\\n    <Route path=\\"settings\\" element={<Settings />} />\\n  </Route>\\n</Routes>\\n\\nconst Layout = () => (\\n  <div>\\n    <header>Header</header>\\n    <Outlet /> {/* Dashboard and Settings render here */}\\n    <footer>Footer</footer>\\n  </div>\\n);"],[2763,"Absolute paths start with `/` and give the full path from the root. Relative paths depend on the current location (URL) and let you build routes relative to the current one.","Absolute paths:\\n\\n- Start with `/`.\\n\\n- Give the exact location of the route regardless of the current path.\\n\\nRelative paths:\\n\\n- Are given relative to the current route.\\n\\n- Are convenient for nested routes.\\n\\nExample: for the current URL `/user/123`:\\n\\n- The absolute path `/settings` leads to `/settings`.\\n\\n- The relative path `settings` leads to `/user/123/settings`.\\n\\nUsing relative paths makes the code more flexible and easier to maintain when the route structure changes.","<Link to=\\"/about\\">About</Link> // Absolute path\\n\\n<Link to=\\"profile\\">Profile</Link> // Relative path"],[2764,"A redirect can be set up with the `<Navigate>` component for automatic redirection, or programmatically through the `useNavigate` hook.","Redirecting with `<Navigate>`:\\nUsed for automatic redirection, for example when a condition is checked.\\n\\nProgrammatic redirect through `useNavigate`:\\nLets you redirect the user on a button press or in the course of running some logic.\\n\\nRedirects are useful for protecting routes, redirecting after an action, or moving users away from outdated pages.","import { Navigate } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst ProtectedRoute = ({ isAuthenticated }) => {\\n  return isAuthenticated ? <Dashboard /> : <Navigate to=\\"/login\\" />;\\n};\\n\\nimport { useNavigate } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Login = () => {\\n  const navigate = useNavigate();\\n  const handleLogin = () => {\\n    // Authentication logic\\n    navigate(\'/dashboard\');\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={handleLogin}>Login</button>;\\n};"],[2765,"The `push` method adds a new route to the browser history, which lets you go back. The `replace` method replaces the current route without keeping it in history, which prevents returning to the previous page.","The `useNavigate` hook provides two ways to navigate, differing in how they manipulate the browser history:\\n\\n- `push` (the default):\\n\\n- Adds a new route to the history stack.\\n\\n- The user can go back using the browser\'s \\"Back\\" button.\\n\\n- `replace`:\\n\\n- Replaces the current route.\\n\\n- Does not add it to the history stack, which rules out going back.\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- `push`: For ordinary navigation, when the user may want to return to the previous page.\\n\\n- `replace`: For redirects, to rule out going back (for example after logging in).","navigate(\'/dashboard\'); // Equivalent to push\\n\\nnavigate(\'/login\', { replace: true });"],[2766,"Fallback routes handle the case where the requested route does not exist. They are implemented with the `*` route and let you show an error page or redirect to the main route.","Fallback routes are routes that fire when none of the previously defined routes matched the current URL. In React Router they are declared with the path `*`.\\nExample implementation:\\n\\nIf the user navigates to a path that does not exist, for example `/unknown`, the `NotFound` component renders.\\nUsing fallback routes improves the user experience, preventing blank pages and directing the user to useful information.","import { Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Routes>\\n    <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n    <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n    <Route path=\\"*\\" element={<NotFound />} /> {/* Fallback route */}\\n  </Routes>\\n);\\nconst NotFound = () => <h1>404: Page Not Found</h1>;"],[2767,"Lazy routing loads route components only when they are needed. This is achieved using `React.lazy` combined with a dynamic import (`import()`), which reduces the initial bundle size.","Lazy routing lets you load route components \\"on demand\\" instead of including them in the initial bundle. This is especially useful for large applications where not every page is needed right away.\\nExample implementation:\\nImport components lazily with `React.lazy`:\\n\\nUse `<Suspense>` to show a loading indicator:\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- When the user navigates to the `/about` route, the `About` component is loaded dynamically.\\n\\n- This reduces the cost of the application\'s initial load.","import { lazy } from \'react\';\\n\\nconst Home = lazy(() => import(\'./Home\'));\\nconst About = lazy(() => import(\'./About\'));\\n\\nimport { Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nimport { Suspense } from \'react\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n      <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n    </Routes>\\n  </Suspense>\\n);"],[2768,"`React.lazy` is used to import components lazily, and `Suspense` lets you show a loading indicator while the component loads.","`React.lazy` and `Suspense` work together to make loading large components or pages easier. The core idea is to load components only when they are genuinely needed.\\nSetting up `React.lazy`:\\nUse the `React.lazy` function for a lazy import:\\n\\nUsing `Suspense`:\\nWrap the routes or components in `<Suspense>` to handle the loading state:\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Components are fetched only when they are used.\\n\\n- The application\'s main page loads faster.\\n\\nExample with nested routes:\\nIt works with nested routes too:\\n\\nSo `lazy` and `Suspense` let you control component loading, minimising the application\'s load time and improving the user experience.","const Profile = lazy(() => import(\'./Profile\'));\\n\\nimport { Suspense } from \'react\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/profile\\" element={<Profile />} />\\n    </Routes>\\n  </Suspense>\\n);\\n\\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import(\'./Dashboard\'));\\nconst Settings = lazy(() => import(\'./Settings\'));\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/dashboard\\" element={<Dashboard />}>\\n        <Route path=\\"settings\\" element={<Settings />} />\\n      </Route>\\n    </Routes>\\n  </Suspense>\\n);"],[2769,"Protected routes let you restrict access to components or pages. They are implemented by checking a condition (for example authentication) and redirecting unauthorised users to another page.","Protected routes are used to prevent access to pages unless certain conditions are met — for example, if the user is not signed in.\\nExample implementation:\\n\\n- Create a guard component:\\n\\n- Use the guard component in the routes:\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Simplifies managing access to routes.\\n\\n- Improves security by ruling out access to restricted resources.","import { Navigate } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst ProtectedRoute = ({ isAuthenticated, children }) => {\\n  return isAuthenticated ? children : <Navigate to=\\"/login\\" />;\\n};\\n\\nimport { Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst App = () => {\\n  const isAuthenticated = /* Authentication check logic */;\\n  return (\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/dashboard\\" element={\\n        <ProtectedRoute isAuthenticated={isAuthenticated}>\\n          <Dashboard />\\n        </ProtectedRoute>\\n      } />\\n      <Route path=\\"/login\\" element={<Login />} />\\n    </Routes>\\n  );\\n};"],[2770,"An access-rights check (authorization) is added to routes where access is granted only for a matching role or permission. It is implemented by checking before the component renders.","Access rights can be checked by adding a condition to a protected route. For example, using roles:\\nA component for checking roles:\\n\\nAn example route with authorization:\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Makes it easy to manage access based on roles.\\n\\n- Provides flexibility when implementing complex security scenarios.","const RoleProtectedRoute = ({ userRole, allowedRoles, children }) => {\\n  return allowedRoles.includes(userRole) ? children : <Navigate to=\\"/unauthorized\\" />;\\n};\\n\\nconst App = () => {\\n  const userRole = \\"admin\\"; // Example of the current role\\n  return (\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/admin\\" element={\\n        <RoleProtectedRoute userRole={userRole} allowedRoles={[\'admin\']}>\\n          <AdminPanel />\\n        </RoleProtectedRoute>\\n      } />\\n      <Route path=\\"/unauthorized\\" element={<Unauthorized />} />\\n    </Routes>\\n  );\\n};"],[2771,"To intercept an attempted route change, React Router provides the `usePrompt` hook (or `useBlocker` in React Router 6), which lets you show a confirmation or run logic before the path changes.","Intercepting attempted route changes can be implemented as follows:\\nUsing the `useBlocker` hook:\\n\\nExample usage:\\n\\nExplanation:\\n\\n- If `isDirty` is `true`, an attempted route change triggers a confirmation.\\n\\n- If the user agrees, the transition continues.\\n\\nImportant: in recent versions of React Router, `useNavigate` is recommended for managing programmatic redirects, but `useBlocker` remains useful for confirmations.","import { useBlocker } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst useUnsavedChangesPrompt = (shouldBlock) => {\\n  useBlocker(({ retry }) => {\\n    if (window.confirm(\\"You have unsaved changes. Leave the page?\\")) {\\n      retry(); // Continue the transition\\n    }\\n  }, shouldBlock);\\n};\\n\\nconst FormPage = () => {\\n  const [isDirty, setIsDirty] = useState(false);\\n  useUnsavedChangesPrompt(isDirty);\\n  const handleInputChange = () => setIsDirty(true);\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      <input onChange={handleInputChange} />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\" onClick={() => setIsDirty(false)}>Save</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n};"],[2772,"`<BrowserRouter>` provides basic routing with routes described declaratively in JSX. `createBrowserRouter` lets you describe routes programmatically, providing advanced capabilities such as data loaders, error handlers and route actions.","`<BrowserRouter>`:\\n\\n- Works through JSX.\\n\\n- Routes are described declaratively:\\n\\n`createBrowserRouter`:\\n\\n- Lets you describe routes as a JavaScript object.\\n\\n- Supports loaders, actions and error handling.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n\\n- `createBrowserRouter` provides more capability for server rendering and managing route state.\\n\\n- `<BrowserRouter>` is simpler and suits applications without complex route logic.","import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <BrowserRouter>\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n      <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n    </Routes>\\n  </BrowserRouter>\\n);\\n\\nimport { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/\\",\\n    element: <Home />,\\n    errorElement: <NotFound />,\\n    loader: fetchHomeData,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/about\\",\\n    element: <About />,\\n  },\\n]);\\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;"],[2773,"For server-side routing you need to configure the server (for example Node.js with Express) so that it handles every request and points it at the React application\'s main HTML file. React Router on the client then takes over rendering components based on the path.","Setting up routes on the server:\\nUse Node.js and Express:\\n\\nWhy this matters:\\n\\n- The server returns the same HTML file for every request.\\n\\n- React Router interprets the URL and renders the matching component.\\n\\nFor server-side rendering (SSR):\\n\\n- Use libraries such as `react-router-dom/server` and `react-dom/server` to render components on the server.\\n\\nIn summary: setting up routes on the server provides deep-link support and prevents 404 errors when a page is accessed directly.","const express = require(\'express\');\\nconst path = require(\'path\');\\nconst app = express();\\n// Serving static files\\napp.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, \'build\')));\\n// Handling every request\\napp.get(\'*\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.sendFile(path.join(__dirname, \'build\', \'index.html\'));\\n});\\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log(\'Server running on port 3000\'));\\n\\nimport { StaticRouter } from \'react-router-dom/server\';\\nconst html = renderToString(\\n  <StaticRouter location={req.url}>\\n    <App />\\n  </StaticRouter>\\n);"],[2774,"Error handling in React Router is done through a route\'s `errorElement` property or through a global error handler at the router level. Errors can be displayed using an error component.","Using the `errorElement` property:\\nAdd `errorElement` to show an error page when a problem occurs.\\n\\nHandling loader errors:\\nIf an error occurs in a data loader, `errorElement` is displayed automatically.\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Centralised error handling.\\n\\n- Better UX, showing clear messages instead of blank pages.","import { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/\\",\\n    element: <Home />,\\n    errorElement: <NotFound />,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/about\\",\\n    element: <About />,\\n  },\\n]);\\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;\\n\\nconst loaderWithError = async () => {\\n  throw new Error(\\"Data loading error\\");\\n};\\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/\\",\\n    element: <Home />,\\n    loader: loaderWithError,\\n    errorElement: <ErrorPage />,\\n  },\\n]);"],[2775,"Integrating React Router DOM with Redux lets you use Redux state to drive routing, for example for redirects based on the user\'s state. This is achieved through hooks such as `useSelector` and `useDispatch` inside route components.","An example with an authentication check through Redux:\\nCreate a Redux store holding user state:\\n\\nConnect Redux to the React application:\\n\\nUsing Redux state in routes:\\nCheck the state before rendering protected routes:\\n\\nIntegrating with redirects:\\nUse `useDispatch` to change routes programmatically:\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Centralises state management and routing.\\n\\n- Makes redirects based on global state convenient.","const initialState = { isAuthenticated: false };\\nconst authReducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case \'LOGIN\':\\n      return { ...state, isAuthenticated: true };\\n    case \'LOGOUT\':\\n      return { ...state, isAuthenticated: false };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nimport { Provider } from \'react-redux\';\\nimport { createStore } from \'redux\';\\nconst store = createStore(authReducer);\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Provider store={store}>\\n    <MainRouter />\\n  </Provider>\\n);\\n\\nimport { useSelector } from \'react-redux\';\\nimport { Navigate } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst ProtectedRoute = ({ children }) => {\\n  const isAuthenticated = useSelector(state => state.isAuthenticated);\\n  return isAuthenticated ? children : <Navigate to=\\"/login\\" />;\\n};\\n\\nimport { useDispatch } from \'react-redux\';\\nimport { useNavigate } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst LoginPage = () => {\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  const navigate = useNavigate();\\n  const handleLogin = () => {\\n    dispatch({ type: \'LOGIN\' });\\n    navigate(\'/dashboard\');\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={handleLogin}>Log in</button>;\\n};"],[2776,"Transition animations between routes in React Router DOM are implemented using the `react-transition-group` library or frameworks such as Framer Motion. Route components are wrapped in an animated container.","Installing `react-transition-group`:\\n\\nAn example with animation:\\nSet up animated routes:\\n\\nAdd the CSS for the animation:\\n\\nUsing Framer Motion:\\nA more flexible approach:\\n\\nIn summary: animating routes makes the application feel smoother and more interactive.","npm install react-transition-group\\n\\nimport { CSSTransition, TransitionGroup } from \'react-transition-group\';\\nimport { Routes, Route, useLocation } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nimport \'./animations.css\';\\nconst AnimatedRoutes = () => {\\n  const location = useLocation();\\n  return (\\n    <TransitionGroup>\\n      <CSSTransition key={location.key} classNames=\\"fade\\" timeout={300}>\\n        <Routes location={location}>\\n          <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n          <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n        </Routes>\\n      </CSSTransition>\\n    </TransitionGroup>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n.fade-enter {\\n  opacity: 0;\\n}\\n.fade-enter-active {\\n  opacity: 1;\\n  transition: opacity 300ms;\\n}\\n.fade-exit {\\n  opacity: 1;\\n}\\n.fade-exit-active {\\n  opacity: 0;\\n  transition: opacity 300ms;\\n}\\n\\nimport { motion } from \'framer-motion\';\\nconst AnimatedPage = ({ children }) => (\\n  <motion.div\\n    initial={{ opacity: 0 }}\\n    animate={{ opacity: 1 }}\\n    exit={{ opacity: 0 }}\\n  >\\n    {children}\\n  </motion.div>\\n);"],[2777,"React Router DOM routes invalid URLs to an error-handling component, which is configured with a special route (`path=\\"*\\"` or `errorElement`).","Using the fallback route `path=\\"*\\"`:\\nAdd a route for unknown URLs:\\n\\nUsing `errorElement`:\\nIn `createBrowserRouter` you can configure a global error handler:\\n\\nShowing a 404 page:\\nCreate a component for the error:\\n\\nWhy this matters:\\nHandling invalid routes improves UX by showing the user useful information instead of a blank page.","import { Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Routes>\\n    <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n    <Route path=\\"*\\" element={<NotFound />} />\\n  </Routes>\\n);\\n\\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/\\",\\n    element: <Home />,\\n    errorElement: <NotFound />,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/about\\",\\n    element: <About />,\\n  },\\n]);\\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;\\n\\nconst NotFound = () => <h1>Page not found</h1>;"],[2778,"`loader` in data routes is used to fetch data before the component renders. It returns data which is then available inside the component through the `useLoaderData` hook.","Defining a `loader`:\\nIt is a function that fetches data asynchronously.\\n\\nExample of using `loader`:\\nDefine a route with a `loader`:\\n\\nReading the data in the component:\\nUse the `useLoaderData` hook:\\n\\nError handling:\\n\\n- If the `loader` throws, `errorElement` takes over.\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Shorter render time, because the data is fetched before the component is shown.\\n\\n- Centralised handling of route data.","const fetchData = async () => {\\n  const response = await fetch(\'/api/data\');\\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(\'Failed to load data\');\\n  return response.json();\\n};\\n\\nimport { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/\\",\\n    element: <Home />,\\n    loader: fetchData,\\n  },\\n]);\\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;\\n\\nimport { useLoaderData } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Home = () => {\\n  const data = useLoaderData();\\n  return <div>{JSON.stringify(data)}</div>;\\n};"],[2779,"`action` handles POST, PUT, DELETE and other requests for a route. It is used to perform operations such as submitting a form or updating data, and returns the operation\'s result, available in the component.","Defining an `action`:\\nIt is a function called on a POST or PUT request to the corresponding route.\\n\\nExample of using `action`:\\nSet up a route with an `action`:\\n\\nHandling form submission:\\nUse `<Form>` from React Router:\\n\\nReading the result:\\nThe operation\'s result is available through `useActionData`.\\n\\nIn summary: `action` simplifies handling requests that change data, and integrates with routing.","const saveData = async ({ request }) => {\\n  const formData = await request.formData();\\n  const response = await fetch(\'/api/save\', {\\n    method: \'POST\',\\n    body: JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(formData)),\\n    headers: { \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\' },\\n  });\\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(\'Failed to save data\');\\n  return response.json();\\n};\\n\\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/form\\",\\n    element: <Form />,\\n    action: saveData,\\n  },\\n]);\\n\\nimport { Form } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst FormComponent = () => (\\n  <Form method=\\"post\\">\\n    <input name=\\"name\\" type=\\"text\\" />\\n    <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n  </Form>\\n);\\n\\nimport { useActionData } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Form = () => {\\n  const result = useActionData();\\n  return result ? <p>Data saved</p> : null;\\n};"],[2780,"`useRouteError` lets you read an error raised in a `loader` or `action` and show it to the user. It is used inside a route\'s `errorElement` to render custom error pages.","How `useRouteError` works:\\nAn error thrown in a `loader` or `action` is passed automatically to the route\'s `errorElement`.\\nInside `errorElement`, the `useRouteError` hook provides the error object.\\nExample usage:\\nSet up a route with error handling:\\n\\nCreate a component to handle the error:\\n\\nHandling action errors:\\nJust like with `loader`, errors from `action` are also handled through `errorElement`.\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Centralised error handling.\\n\\n- The ability to give the user clear error messages.\\n\\nIn summary: `useRouteError` improves the user experience by letting you show detailed information when errors occur.","const router = createBrowserRouter([\\n  {\\n    path: \\"/\\",\\n    element: <Home />,\\n    loader: async () => {\\n      throw new Error(\\"Data loading error\\");\\n    },\\n    errorElement: <ErrorPage />,\\n  },\\n]);\\n\\nimport { useRouteError } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst ErrorPage = () => {\\n  const error = useRouteError();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Error!</h1>\\n      <p>{error.message}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2781,"React Router DOM lets you manage the page title through `document.title` inside a route component. The `useEffect` hook is used to update the title depending on the route.","Using `useEffect` to manage titles:\\nIn the route component, change the page title on render:\\n\\nBenefits:\\n\\n- Dynamic title management for each route.\\n\\n- Easy to implement using React Router DOM.\\n\\nDrawbacks:\\nIn some cases more code may be needed for complex routes, especially when using dynamic data.\\nIn summary: managing titles through `useEffect` together with `useLocation` in React Router DOM provides both flexibility and simplicity.","import { useEffect } from \'react\';\\nimport { useLocation } from \'react-router-dom\';\\nconst Page = () => {\\n  const location = useLocation();\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    document.title = `Page Title - ${location.pathname}`;\\n  }, [location.pathname]);\\n  return <div>Page content</div>;\\n};"],[2782,"React Router DOM provides greater flexibility and integration with any React-based framework or library, whereas Next.js uses built-in routing and can offer server-side optimisations.","Advantages of React Router DOM:\\n\\n- Flexibility: It can be integrated with any framework or library, and is not limited to React alone.\\n\\n- Configuration: It lets you set up routes through JSX and a component-based approach.\\n\\n- Dynamic state management: It supports Redux and other state management systems.\\n\\n- Performance: It lets you configure optimisation and caching on the client side.\\n\\nAdvantages of Next.js routing:\\n\\n- Server-side rendering (SSR): Gives a significant improvement in SEO and performance with static rendering.\\n\\n- Quick setup: Built-in routing lets you create routed applications quickly.\\n\\n- Application-level optimisation: Offers automatic generation of routes and of some components on the server.\\n\\nDisadvantages:\\n\\n- React Router DOM: Does not provide automatic server-side optimisation (SSR) and may need configuration to improve performance.\\n\\n- Next.js: Limits flexibility when integrating with non-Next components and libraries, since routes and components must be set up within the framework.\\n\\nUse in real scenarios:\\n\\n- For most projects React Router DOM is suitable, thanks to its flexibility and its ability to integrate with other libraries.\\n\\n- For applications with demanding SEO and performance requirements, Next.js may be the preferable choice.\\n\\nIn summary: the choice between React Router DOM and Next.js depends on the project\'s specific requirements, such as SEO, performance and integration flexibility.",null],[2783,"When route parameters change, the component may not be recreated. To refresh the data, use a `watch` on `this.$route.params` or the `beforeRouteUpdate` hook. In those places you can request the data again. This approach guarantees the interface matches the current URL. It is standard practice in Vue applications.","In Vue Router, when route parameters such as `id` change, the component often stays the same, so the creation hooks are not called.\\nWhy the component is not recreated\\nVue Router reuses the component if:\\n\\n- the same route is used\\n\\n- only the `params` change\\n\\nThis improves performance but requires refreshing the data manually.\\nUsing watch\\nThe simplest way is to watch the route parameters.\\n\\nSuitable if:\\n\\n- you need to react to a specific parameter\\n\\n- the logic is simple\\n\\nUsing beforeRouteUpdate\\nFor more controlled behaviour, a navigation hook is used.\\n\\nAdvantages:\\n\\n- it is called before navigation completes\\n\\n- both the new and the old route are available\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Load the data in `created` on first entry\\n\\n- Refresh the data through `watch` or `beforeRouteUpdate`\\n\\n- Avoid logic that depends on the component being recreated\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo react to a change in `params` you must track that change explicitly, because the component is not recreated automatically","watch: {\\n  \'$route.params.id\'(newId, oldId) {\\n    this.fetchData(newId);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nbeforeRouteUpdate(to, from, next) {\\n  this.fetchData(to.params.id);\\n  next();\\n}"],[2784,"A change of the dynamic `id` can be handled through a `watch` on `$route.params`, through `beforeRouteUpdate`, or through the `key` attribute on `<router-view>`. Each way has its own characteristics. `watch` and `beforeRouteUpdate` refresh the data without recreating the component. Using `key` causes the component to be recreated.","A dynamic `id` in a route is a common scenario when displaying detail pages.\\nWay 1. watch on params\\nSuitable for refreshing data without recreating the component.\\n\\nPros:\\n\\n- a simple and clear approach\\n\\n- component state is preserved\\n\\nWay 2. beforeRouteUpdate\\nUsed when you need control over navigation.\\n\\nPros:\\n\\n- access to both routes\\n\\n- the ability to cancel navigation\\n\\nWay 3. key on router-view\\nYou can force the component to be recreated.\\n\\nCharacteristics:\\n\\n- the creation hooks are called\\n\\n- component state is reset\\n\\n- rarely used\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice depends on whether component state needs to be preserved or the component recreated when the `id` changes.","watch: {\\n  \'$route.params.id\'(id) {\\n    this.loadData(id);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nbeforeRouteUpdate(to, from, next) {\\n  this.loadData(to.params.id);\\n  next();\\n}\\n\\n<router-view :key=\\"$route.fullPath\\" />"],[2785,"`beforeRouteEnter` is used to run logic before the component is displayed. In this hook there is no access to `this` yet. It is often used to load data before entering the page. Once navigation completes, you can access the component instance through a callback. That makes the hook convenient for initialisation.","`beforeRouteEnter` is a component route guard that is called before the component instance is created.\\nThe peculiarity of accessing this\\nIn `beforeRouteEnter`:\\n\\n- the component instance has not been created yet\\n\\n- `this` is unavailable\\n\\n- access to the component is possible through a callback\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nWhen to use beforeRouteEnter\\nThis hook is suitable if:\\n\\n- you need to load data before rendering\\n\\n- it is important to avoid an intermediate state\\n\\n- control is required before entering the route\\n\\nDifference from beforeRouteUpdate\\n\\n- `beforeRouteEnter` — on first entry\\n\\n- `beforeRouteUpdate` — when params change\\n\\nPractical scenarios\\n\\n- Preloading data\\n\\n- Checking access rights\\n\\n- Logging entry to the page\\n\\nConclusion\\n`beforeRouteEnter` is used to run logic before the component is created, and is useful when the data must be ready before the interface is displayed.","beforeRouteEnter(to, from, next) {\\n  next(vm => {\\n    vm.fetchData();\\n  });\\n}"],[2786,"An authorization check is usually implemented through a global `beforeEach` guard. It checks for a token or an authentication state. If access is missing, it redirects to the login page. This approach centralises the security logic. It is the standard solution in Vue applications.","Controlling access to routes is one of the most common tasks when using Vue Router.\\nUsing the global beforeEach\\nThe most widespread way is a global guard.\\n\\nUsing meta fields\\nRoutes are marked with access flags.\\n\\nAdvantages of the approach\\n\\n- Centralised logic\\n\\n- Easy to maintain\\n\\n- Easy to scale\\n\\nAdditional measures\\n\\n- Checking role permissions\\n\\n- Refreshing the token\\n\\n- Handling the redirect after login\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn authorization check during navigation is implemented through route guards, most often with a global `beforeEach`, which provides access control at the routing level.","router.beforeEach((to, from, next) => {\\n  const isAuth = store.state.isAuthenticated;\\n\\n  if (to.meta.requiresAuth && !isAuth) {\\n    next(\'/login\');\\n  } else {\\n    next();\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n{\\n  path: \'/profile\',\\n  component: Profile,\\n  meta: { requiresAuth: true }\\n}"],[2787,"A dynamic base path lets a React application work correctly when it is deployed not at the domain root but in a subdirectory (for example /app/). To do this you need to configure the router and the bundler. In React Router, use the `basename` prop on the `` component. The basename value can come from environment variables or a configuration file. You also need to configure Webpack or another bundler by setting `publicPath`, so that static assets (JS, CSS) load from the correct path.","Support for a dynamic base path is needed when your React application is deployed not at the domain root (for example `https://example.com`) but in a subdirectory (for example `https://example.com/my-app/`). This is common in microservice architectures where several applications live on one domain, or on shared hosting. Without correct configuration the application will look for assets and routes at the wrong paths, which leads to 404 errors and broken navigation.\\n\\nConfiguring React Router\\n\\nThe main tool for managing paths in React is the router. In React Router (v6) you can set the base path with the `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` or `<Router>` component. The value should be a string matching the subdirectory where your application lives. To make it dynamic, you can read the value from an environment variable or a configuration file loaded at build or run time.\\n\\nConfiguring the bundler (Webpack)\\n\\nFor static assets (JavaScript, CSS, images) to load correctly, you need to configure `publicPath` in your bundler. In Webpack this is done in the configuration. The `publicPath` value must match your application\'s base path. It can also be made dynamic using environment variables.\\n\\nIf you use Create React App, you can set the `PUBLIC_URL` environment variable before building. For example, `PUBLIC_URL=/my-app npm run build`. This automatically sets `publicPath` in Webpack and is used to prefix asset paths in the HTML.\\n\\nWorking with links and API requests\\n\\nWhen using a base path, all relative links to assets (for example images) and API endpoints must take that prefix into account. For links inside the application, use the `<Link>` component from React Router, which accounts for `basename` automatically. For API requests it is recommended to use absolute URLs or to create a base axios/fetch instance with the prefix set.\\n\\nConclusion: a dynamic base path is worth implementing when your React application is going to be deployed in a subdirectory. It ensures routing works correctly and assets load in environments such as microservices, corporate portals or shared hosting.","import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from \'react-router-dom\';\\n\\n// The base path value can come from an environment variable or config.\\nconst BASE_PATH = process.env.REACT_APP_BASE_PATH || \'/\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter basename={BASE_PATH}>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<HomePage />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<AboutPage />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  output: {\\n    publicPath: process.env.PUBLIC_PATH || \'/\',\\n  },\\n  // ... the rest of the configuration\\n};\\n\\n// An example of configuring axios with a base URL\\nimport axios from \'axios\';\\n\\nconst api = axios.create({\\n  baseURL: process.env.REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL || \'/api\',\\n});\\n\\n// Every request through this instance will have the correct prefix.\\napi.get(\'/users\').then(response => console.log(response));"],[2788,"The base URL in React Router is a prefix automatically added to every relative route in the application. It is needed when your React application is deployed not at the domain root (for example at `example.com/my-app/`) but in a subdirectory. Without the correct base URL, routing can break, because React Router will look for paths relative to the server root rather than your subdirectory. You set it with the `basename` prop on the `` component. This makes links and navigation work correctly in a production environment.","In web development, especially when deploying single-page applications (SPAs), it often happens that the application is placed not in the domain\'s root directory but inside a subfolder. For example, it may be available at `https://company.com/dashboard/` or `https://github.com/username/project-name/` (for GitHub Pages). By default React Router assumes the application runs from the root (`/`). If that is not the case, all relative routes (for example `/users` or `/settings`) will be resolved relative to the server root, which leads to 404 errors or components rendering incorrectly.\\n\\nWhat is basename?\\n\\nThe `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` (or `<HashRouter>`) component lets you specify a base path for the whole application. Every route defined inside that router will automatically carry this prefix. This affects:\\n\\n- Link generation by the `<Link to=\\"...\\">` component.\\n\\n- Path matching in `<Route path=\\"...\\">`.\\n\\n- The behaviour of hooks such as `useNavigate()` and `useLocation()`.\\n\\nHow do you use it?\\n\\nYou set `basename` when creating the router. Here is a practical example:\\n\\nIn this example, if the user goes to `/admin/users`, React Router will correctly render the `UsersList` component, because it knows the base path is `/admin`.\\n\\nImportant details\\n\\n- Server configuration: setting `basename` in React Router does not replace configuring the server. Your web server (Nginx, Apache) or host (GitHub Pages, Netlify) must also be configured so that every request within `/your-base-path` is routed to your `index.html` (the SPA \\"catch-all\\" rule).\\n\\n- Absolute paths: if you use absolute URLs (starting with `/`) inside `basename`, they will be completed automatically. Relative paths (without a leading slash) work relative to the current URL.\\n\\n- Hash router: for `HashRouter` the principle is the same, but the hash fragment (`#`) is added after the base path.\\n\\nConclusion: use `basename` in React Router when your application is deployed in a subdirectory on the server. It is a necessary condition for routing and navigation to work correctly and for avoiding 404 errors in a production environment.","import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from \'react-router-dom\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  // The application is deployed in the /admin subdirectory\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter basename=\\"/admin\\">\\n      <nav>\\n        <Link to=\\"/\\">Dashboard</Link> // Actual path: /admin/\\n        <Link to=\\"/users\\">Users</Link> // Actual path: /admin/users\\n      </nav>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Dashboard />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"/users\\" element={<UsersList />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}"],[2789,"Client-side routing in an SPA handles transitions between pages in the browser without reloading the page, using JavaScript and the History API. Server navigation sends a request to the server, which returns new HTML, causing a full reload. Client-side routing is faster and creates a smooth user experience, but requires loading the whole application up front. Server navigation is simpler for SEO and works without JavaScript.","The main difference\\nClient-side routing in an SPA (Single Page Application) handles navigation in the browser without a full page reload. Server navigation, by contrast, sends a request to the server on every transition, and the server returns a new HTML document, causing a full reload.\\nHow client-side routing works\\nIn an SPA the application loads once, and then JavaScript intercepts clicks on links, changes the URL using the History API (pushState or replaceState) and updates the page content dynamically. This avoids the delays involved in loading resources again.\\n\\nHow server navigation works\\nWith server navigation, every transition is a new HTTP request. The server handles the URL, generates HTML and sends it to the client. The browser fully reloads the page, resetting the application state.\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nClient-side routing suits interactive applications where responsiveness and smooth transitions matter (for example dashboards, social networks). Server navigation is better for content sites where SEO optimisation and a fast first render matter (for example blogs, news portals).\\nConclusion: the choice between client-side and server routing depends on requirements for performance, SEO and application complexity. For highly interactive SPAs, client-side routing is preferable; for content sites, server routing.","// An example with React Router\\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from \'react-router-dom\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter>\\n      <nav>\\n        <Link to=\\"/\\">Home</Link>\\n        <Link to=\\"/about\\">About</Link>\\n      </nav>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// An example of a server route in Express\\napp.get(\'/about\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.send(\'<h1>About Page</h1><p>Server-rendered content</p>\');\\n});"],[2790,"React Router v6 introduced several significant changes. The Switch component was replaced by Routes, and Route now uses element instead of component. Route order no longer matters, because v6 uses a ranking algorithm. The useHistory hook was replaced by useNavigate. v6 also added support for nested routes and relative links.","The main differences between React Router v5 and v6\\nReact Router v6 is a significant update that simplifies the API and makes routing more intuitive. The main change is replacing the Switch component with Routes, which automatically picks the best-matching route rather than the first match.\\nChanges in route components\\nIn v5, the Switch component was used to group Route elements, and route order mattered. In v6, Switch is replaced by Routes, and Route now takes an element prop instead of component or render. This lets you pass components directly:\\n\\nNavigation and hooks\\nIn v5, the useHistory hook was used for programmatic navigation. In v6 it is replaced by useNavigate, which provides a more flexible API:\\n\\nNested routes and relative links\\nReact Router v6 significantly improved support for nested routes. Child routes can now be defined directly inside the parent component using Outlet:\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Router v6 offers a cleaner, more predictable API, simplifies working with nested routes and improves performance. When migrating from v5 to v6 it is worth paying attention to replacing Switch with Routes, updating the navigation hooks, and refactoring nested routes to use Outlet.","// React Router v5\\n<Switch>\\n  <Route path=\\"/home\\" component={Home} />\\n  <Route path=\\"/about\\" component={About} />\\n</Switch>\\n\\n// React Router v6\\n<Routes>\\n  <Route path=\\"/home\\" element={<Home />} />\\n  <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n</Routes>\\n\\n// React Router v5\\nconst history = useHistory();\\nhistory.push(\'/home\');\\n\\n// React Router v6\\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\\nnavigate(\'/home\');\\nnavigate(-1); // to go back\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Routes>\\n      <Route path=\\"/dashboard\\" element={<Dashboard />}>\\n        <Route path=\\"stats\\" element={<Stats />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"settings\\" element={<Settings />} />\\n      </Route>\\n    </Routes>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Dashboard() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Dashboard</h1>\\n      <Outlet /> {/* Child routes will render here */}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2791,"Next.js provides built-in solutions for rendering on the server (SSR), the static generation of pages (SSG), routing, the optimization of images and API routes, which speeds development up and improves SEO and performance in comparison with configuring those systems by hand in a React application.","Next.js is a framework built on top of React, which solves the common tasks of web development \\"out of the box\\".\\nThe key advantages:\\n\\n- Built-in SSR and SSG:\\n\\n- Removes the need to configure complex Webpack configurations or Node.js servers for server-side rendering.\\n\\n- Makes it easier to create hybrid applications, where some pages are rendered on the server and others on the client.\\n\\n- File-based routing:\\n\\n- The routing is based on the structure of the folders in the project (`pages/` or `app/`), which is intuitive and reduces the amount of boilerplate code.\\n\\n- Supports dynamic routes and nested layouts.\\n\\n- Optimizations \\"out of the box\\":\\n\\n- Code splitting: breaks the code into chunks automatically for a fast load.\\n\\n- The optimization of images: the `next/image` component compresses images automatically, converts them into modern formats and lazy-loads them.\\n\\n- Fonts: the optimization and loading of web fonts.\\n\\n- Styles: support for CSS-in-JS, CSS modules, Sass and Tailwind CSS.\\n\\n- API Routes:\\n\\n- Lets you write backend logic (API endpoints) in the same project as the frontend, without standing a separate server up.\\n\\n- An improved Developer Experience (DX):\\n\\n- Hot Code Reloading.\\n\\n- Fast builds and advanced error handling.\\n\\nWhen to choose Next.js rather than React:\\n\\n- For sites where SEO matters (blogs, online shops, media).\\n\\n- When high performance by way of SSR/SSG is needed.\\n\\n- To speed development up by way of the built-in solutions.",null],[2792,"SSR is the process of rendering a React application into HTML on the server on every request. That improves SEO, since the search bots see finished content, and speeds the initial load of the page up for the user, because the browser immediately receives markup ready to be displayed.","How SSR works in Next.js:\\n\\n- The user requests a page.\\n\\n- The server on Node.js runs the React components corresponding to that path.\\n\\n- React renders those components into a string of HTML.\\n\\n- The server sends the fully finished HTML document, the CSS and the JavaScript to the client.\\n\\n- The browser displays the HTML, then React \\"hydrates\\" the page, making it interactive.\\n\\nThe key advantages:\\n\\n- SEO (Search Engine Optimization):\\n\\n- Search systems (Google, Yandex) and social networks (Facebook, Twitter) index the content without trouble, since they receive full HTML.\\n\\n- The initial load (First Load):\\n\\n- The user sees the content faster, especially on slow devices or connections, since the browser does not have to wait for all of the JS to load and run in order to paint it.\\n\\n- Social previews (Open Graph):\\n\\n- Social networks display the page\'s preview properly, since they can read the meta tags from the HTML.\\n\\nThe disadvantages:\\n\\n- An increased load on the server, since it renders every page for every request.\\n\\n- A more complex architecture and deployment in comparison with purely static sites.\\n\\nWhen to use SSR:\\n\\n- For pages with dynamic data that changes often (a news feed, personal recommendations).\\n\\n- When SEO and social previews are critically important.",null],[2793,"SSG is the process of building the HTML for pages at build time. Those static files are then served by a CDN. It is the fastest method, which suits content that does not change on every request ideally — blogs, documentation and marketing pages, for example.","How SSG works in Next.js:\\n\\n- During the `next build` command, Next.js runs the React components for the pages marked for SSG.\\n\\n- The result of the rendering (HTML, JSON) is saved in the form of static files.\\n\\n- Those files are placed on a CDN and given to the browser instantly on request.\\n\\nThe key advantages:\\n\\n- Maximum performance:\\n\\n- The pages load instantly, since finished files are given out without any server-side logic.\\n\\n- Cheaper and simpler to host:\\n\\n- Static files can be hosted on any CDN or server (Vercel, Netlify, S3), which is often free or very cheap.\\n\\n- A high resilience to load:\\n\\n- A CDN gives out thousands of requests a second with ease.\\n\\n- Better safety:\\n\\n- The absence of server-side logic reduces the surface for attacks.\\n\\nWhen to use SSG (the ideal cases):\\n\\n- Marketing sites (landing pages)\\n\\n- Blogs and documentation (the content is updated infrequently)\\n\\n- Product catalogues (if the data is updated once a day)\\n\\n- Portfolios\\n\\nRevalidation (ISR — Incremental Static Regeneration):\\nNext.js lets you update static pages after the build at certain intervals of time without having to rebuild the whole site.",null],[2794,"In the old Pages Router, a special `getServerSideProps` function was used for fetching data on the server, which passed the data into the page component through props. In the new App Router, Server Components (marked as `async`) can fetch data directly by means of `fetch` or other libraries and then render JSX.","1. The Pages Router (`getServerSideProps`):\\nThis is a special function that had to be exported from the page\'s file. It ran on the server on every request.\\n\\n2. The App Router (async Server Components):\\nIn the App Router the component function itself can be asynchronous. That simplifies the logic, making the fetching of data straightforward.\\n\\nThe key differences:\\n\\n- The App Router is more intuitive; such logic is simpler to write and to read.\\n\\n- The App Router lets you combine components that fetch data with ordinary components more flexibly.\\n\\n- The Pages Router separated the data logic from the component explicitly.","// pages/blog/[slug].js (the Pages Router)\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const { params } = context;\\n  const postData = await getPostData(params.slug); // A request to an API or a DB\\n\\n  return {\\n    props: {\\n      postData, // The data is passed into the page component as props\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nexport default function BlogPost({ postData }) { // We receive the data through props\\n  return (\\n    <article>\\n      <h1>{postData.title}</h1>\\n      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: postData.content }} />\\n    </article>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// app/blog/[slug]/page.js (the App Router)\\nexport default async function BlogPost({ params }) {\\n  // We make the asynchronous request right in the component\\n  const postData = await getPostData(params.slug);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <article>\\n      <h1>{postData.title}</h1>\\n      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: postData.content }} />\\n    </article>\\n  );\\n}"],[2795,"Yes, absolutely. In Next.js you can and should address databases or internal APIs directly from Server Components, Server Actions or API Routes. The access keys and the logic stay on the server, never reaching the client code, which ensures safety","Next.js is a full-fledged full-stack framework, which lets you write backend logic alongside the frontend.\\nThe ways of working with data on the server:\\n\\n- Directly in Server Components:\\n\\n- You can import and use an ORM (such as Prisma) or DB drivers right in the component.\\n\\n- That is safe, since the code never reaches the browser.\\n\\n- Through API Routes:\\n\\n- Create an endpoint in the folder `app/api/route.js`.\\n\\n- It can be addressed from client components too, through `fetch`.\\n\\n- Through Server Actions:\\n\\n- They let you call asynchronous functions on the server right from client components (when submitting a form, for example).\\n\\nImportant for safety:\\n\\n- Never connect to a DB from Client Components. Do it only in Server Components, API Routes or Server Actions.\\n\\n- Hold every secret key (API keys, connection strings) in environment variables, to which the client has no access.","// app/users/page.js\\nimport prisma from \'@/lib/prisma\'; // We import the DB client\\n\\nexport default async function UsersPage() {\\n  const users = await prisma.user.findMany(); // A direct request to the DB\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {users.map((user) => (\\n        <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2796,"For scripts for which the earliest possible execution is critical, the `next/script` component with the `beforeInteractive` strategy is used. It inserts the script into the `<head>` element of the original HTML document, and it runs before the hydration of the React application.","The `next/script` component is an extension of the ordinary HTML `<script>` tag, which provides control over when and how third-party scripts are loaded.\\nThe loading strategies (`strategy`):\\n\\n- `beforeInteractive`: for scripts that have to run before the page becomes interactive (before React\'s hydration). It is the earliest moment.\\n\\n- Examples: scripts for accessibility testing, bots, certain kinds of analytics.\\n\\n- The placement: inserted into the `<head>` of the server-side HTML.\\n\\n- `afterInteractive` (the default): for scripts that can be run after the page has become interactive.\\n\\n- Examples: most analytics scripts (Google Analytics, Yandex.Metrika).\\n\\n- The placement: inserted at the end of the `<body>` and run after the hydration.\\n\\n- `lazyOnload`: for scripts that can be loaded in the background, once all of the other resources have already loaded.\\n\\n- Examples: scripts for chat widgets, reviews.\\n\\n- The placement: loaded in idle time.\\n\\nAn example of using `beforeInteractive`:\\n\\nImportant: use `beforeInteractive` with caution and only for scripts that really are critical, since they can block the main thread and slow the load down.","// In the root layout (app/layout.js)\\nimport Script from \'next/script\';\\n\\nexport default function RootLayout({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <html lang=\\"en\\">\\n      <body>\\n        {/* This script will run as early as possible */}\\n        <Script\\n          src=\\"https://example.com/critical-script.js\\"\\n          strategy=\\"beforeInteractive\\"\\n        />\\n        {children}\\n      </body>\\n    </html>\\n  );\\n}"],[2797,"Middleware in Next.js is code that runs on an edge server (or on Node.js) before a request reaches your page or API route. It is often used for authentication, checking access rights, rewriting URL paths, working with cookies and localization.","Middleware works as an intermediate layer between an incoming request and your application.\\nThe widespread scenarios for its use:\\n\\n- Authentication and authorization:\\n\\n- Checking a JWT token from a cookie or a header.\\n\\n- Redirecting unauthorized users to the login page.\\n\\n- Localization (i18n):\\n\\n- Determining the user\'s language and redirecting them to the corresponding subdomain or path (`/en/about`, `/ru/about`).\\n\\n- Protecting routes:\\n\\n- Blocking access to the admin panel for users without the rights.\\n\\n- Checking CORS for API routes.\\n\\n- A/B testing and redirects:\\n\\n- Showing different versions of a page to different users based on cookies or other parameters.\\n\\n- Logging and analytics:\\n\\n- Recording information about the requests.\\n\\nThe technical details:\\n\\n- Middleware works on the Edge Runtime by default (faster, but with limitations) or on the Node.js Runtime.\\n\\n- The middleware file has to be called `middleware.js` and be located in the root of the project or in the `src` folder.\\n\\n- You can configure matchers in order to filter the requests to which the middleware is applied.","// middleware.js\\nimport { NextResponse } from \'next/server\';\\nexport function middleware(request) {\\n  const token = request.cookies.get(\'auth-token\');\\n  if (!token) {\\n    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL(\'/login\', request.url));\\n  }\\n  // If there is a token, we let the request through\\n  return NextResponse.next();\\n}"],[2798,"Server components cannot use state (`useState`), effects (`useEffect`) or other life-cycle hooks, because they are rendered once on the server and do not \\"live\\" in the browser. They also cannot use browser APIs (`window` or `document`, for example) or event handlers.","Server Components are a fundamentally different mental model in comparison with client components. Their main task is to render some static (or data-dependent) markup.\\nWhat you must NOT use in Server Components:\\n\\n- React\'s hooks:\\n\\n- `useState`, `useReducer`, `useEffect`, `useContext` (for state) and others.\\n\\n- The reason: they are tied to the component\'s life cycle in the browser (mounting, updating, unmounting), whereas a server component is rendered once and does not \\"update\\".\\n\\n- Browser APIs:\\n\\n- `window`, `document`, `navigator`, `localStorage`, and so on.\\n\\n- The reason: those objects exist only in the browser\'s environment, whereas a server component runs in the Node.js or Edge Runtime environment.\\n\\n- Event handlers:\\n\\n- `onClick`, `onChange` and other props that expect interactivity.\\n\\n- The reason: events happen in the browser, whereas a server component has already been rendered and sent.\\n\\nWhat you CAN and should do in Server Components:\\n\\n- Fetch data directly from a DB or an API.\\n\\n- Work with the file system (on the server).\\n\\n- Access server-side resources safely (using secret keys).\\n\\n- Render large components without increasing the client\'s bundle file.\\n\\nThe proper division:\\n\\n- Use Server Components for everything that requires no interactivity (data, layouts, static content).\\n\\n- Use Client Components (marked `\'use client\'`) for everything interactive (forms, buttons, state, animations).",null],[2799,"The `next/image` component compresses images automatically, converts them into modern formats (WebP/AVIF), changes their dimensions to suit the layout needed and lazy-loads them. That reduces the weight of the pages and speeds their loading up with no effort on the developer\'s part","The `<Image />` component is a replacement for the ordinary HTML `<img>` tag with powerful optimization \\"under the bonnet\\".\\nThe key optimization functions:\\n\\n- Modern formats:\\n\\n- Next.js converts images automatically into WebP or AVIF (if the browser supports them), which are often much smaller than JPEG or PNG at the same quality.\\n\\n- Resizing \\"on the fly\\":\\n\\n- You specify only the dimensions needed in the layout (`width`, `height`).\\n\\n- Next.js creates and gives out an image of precisely that size, rather than the huge original.\\n\\n- Lazy loading by default:\\n\\n- The images are loaded only when they appear in the viewport, saving traffic and speeding the initial load up.\\n\\n- Prioritizing the load:\\n\\n- By means of the `priority` prop you can mark the images that matter for the Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) (a hero image, for example), so as to load them immediately.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nThe important aspects:\\n\\n- Mandatory dimensions: you have to specify `width` and `height` explicitly in order to prevent Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).\\n\\n- Domains: to optimize images from external URLs you have to add them to `next.config.js` under `images.domains`.\\n\\n- Hosting: the optimization works on any hosting, but is most effective on Vercel.","import Image from \'next/image\';\\n\\nexport default function Hero() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Image\\n        src=\\"/hero.jpg\\" // The path to the image in the `public` folder, or an absolute URL\\n        alt=\\"Description of image\\"\\n        width={800} // The mandatory props\\n        height={600}\\n        priority // We mark this image as a priority\\n      />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2800,"The App Router is the new model based on React Server Components and on the `app/` folder, which offers nested layouts, a simplified fetching of data and richer capabilities. The Pages Router is the older, stable model based on the `pages/` folder, which is well known and is still widely used.","Next.js offers two parallel routing systems.\\nThe Pages Router (the old model):\\n\\n- The structure: the routes are defined by the files in the `pages/` folder. `pages/about.js` -> the route `/about`.\\n\\n- The data: special functions are used (`getServerSideProps`, `getStaticProps`).\\n\\n- Layouts: layouts are implemented by hand, by way of wrapping the components.\\n\\n- The status: fully supported, but receives no new functionality.\\n\\nThe App Router (the new, recommended model):\\n\\n- The structure: the routes are defined by nested folders in `app/`. `app/about/page.js` -> the route `/about`.\\n\\n- The data: Server Components and asynchronous components are used.\\n\\n- Layouts: built-in support for nested layouts through `layout.js` files.\\n\\n- The new capabilities: Server Components, Server Actions, Streaming, Parallel Routes, Intercepting Routes.\\n\\nThe choice:\\n\\n- For new projects it is recommended to use the App Router.\\n\\n- Existing projects on the Pages Router can continue to work and migrate gradually.",null]]');export{e as default};